      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>TirE EARNINGS OF SINGERS.</head>
        <p>TirE EARNINGS OF SINGERS.</p>
        <p>flow unequal are the earnings of singers is only realised vaguely by those who are not actu- ally members of the musical profession. But the fact is brought forward clearly enough by a writer in the Musical World. Speaking of the opera companies which tour the provinces, he says: "One lady who undertakes the part of Carmen may be offered as much as £3O to £3.5 a performance; another Carmen will have to content herself with as little as £4 to £7 a week, in return for which she is expected to appear, say, three nights. The Faust of one opera com- pany receives but a fourth of the sum paid .a Faust of another troupe, and a baritone may be paid a weekly salary which one of Mr. George Edwardes's leading warblers would decline. In fact, so small are the incomes earned by some principals that one wonders how they manage to keep themselves in the necessities of life." Members of the chorus, the same authority , states, receive from £2 15s. to .£l' 10s. a week, "costumes found."</p>
        <p>SMART COATEE.</p>
        <p>The smart little coatees that are now worn se much make useful little wraps. They are gene- rally composed of taffetas in black or pale shades of fawn. Some that are shepn for early summer wear are constructed more for the sake of /appearance than for actual warmth, many people preferring some form of a coat, however light, for outdoor wear. The basis ,of the coatee shewn in the sketch is black taffetas, tucked all over with tiny pin tucks, cut in vandykes at the edge, and finished by narrow frills of taffetas</p>
        <p>and a deep frill of fine black lace. Round the opening at the neck is a narrow frill and a broad soft ribbon of satin. It is gathered into rosettes, and finishes in a large bow across the front, the long ends reaching half way down the skirt. The sleeves are made over a thin lining to the elbow, the full puffs of black lace being tucked up by rosettes of ribbon, and and in frills of lace. The hat worn with this coatee is of fine white crinoline. Round the c,rown is a narrow green velvet ribbon, tied in a quaint little bow in front", and raised at the back with clusters of roses and forget-me-nots. LACE AND EMBROIDERY.</p>
        <p>Lace collar; are for 'the time being out as collars, says a writer in the Globe. There are half-a-dozen other ways in which these pretty things can. be utilised. A favourite, one is in- serting them into muslin blouses as yokes. They were being soldoff at such reasonable prices just before Easter that this adornment of a blouse which renders it at once dressy is sure to be popular. Lace, especially the thicker kind, is used greatly to trim the under part of the brims of hats. There is, indeed, never a tide in the affairs of fashion when lace is not a valuable asset. Imitation is now so excellent that the possession of the real thing is a satisfaction only to the possessor; a satisfaction, moreover,which, is usually mentioned in the course of ordinary conversation. Beautiful, however, as lace is, re- fined and cultured-looking a trimming as it is, embroidery takes first place just now in fashion's favour. The kind known as broderie Anglaise is possibly that which is most in demara for wash- ing blouses are the newest and most approved style of bodice. The days of lace and chiffon and lawn, which were discreetly said to be mellow, and truthfully known to be dirty, are past. Neatness and spotless daintiness are key- notes of the dress fantasia for this summer.</p>
