      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Academy View.—Lady Warwick, fresh from preaching Socialism to factory workers, was amongst the fashionable throng at the private view of the Royal Academg. So also were several other Society women, whose portraits are depicted on the walls. Society scarcely knows how to take Lady Warwick's Socialist convictions, but she of course Inds few sympathisers among such gatherings, which on this occasion included the Duchess of Marlborough, the Duchess of Westminster, the Dowager Duchess of Manchester, Lady Helen Vincent, ady Kilmorley, Lady Colen Campbell, and Mrs. George Cornwallis West. The general disposition is, not to regard Lady Warwick's appearance on the political platform very seriously, but it was naturally a subject of reference at the Academy view. "Lady Warwick defying Society" was the title bestowed upon her portrait, which is generally regarded as one of the most successful pictures of the year, and the Marl- borough group of portraits is another that is attracting much attention. Lady Warwick's Socialist Convictions.—The free maintenance of hungry school children is one of two reforms which the Countess of Warwick puts before all others in her Socialist programme. She is therefore highly gratified that this is practically conceded in the new order of the Local Government Board, which puts upon the community the responsibility of providing for underfed children in the public elementary schools of England and Wales. This involves important and almost revolntionary changes in our ideas of social responsibilities, but they are very welcome to Lady Warwick, who makes no secret of her whole-hearted sympathy with the Socialist propaganda. There is one point with which she disagrees, and that is, the demand i for the abolition of the monarchy. She is n favour of the State confining its teaching to secular subjects, and education is one cf the principal topics in the electioneering campaign she is carrying on in various constituencies, on behalf ,of the Labour Parliamentary candidates. A New Factor in Romance.—ln a thoutand love stories, the heroine and heiress has married the coach- man or the groom, and now the chauffeur is losing no time in making himself a new factor in the romance of matrimony. Only the other day Mr. Carnegie's niece was married to a cardriver in America, and this event was followed by the marriage in London of a niece of Lady Kilmorley, to the man who had occupied the position of chauffeur to her father. Every effort MIS made on the part of her relations to dissuade her from the marriage, but the young lady had a large and inde- pendent fortune of her own, and took her own course in the matter. Hitherto, the novelist has not had the courage to make a hero of the skilled mechanic, in his love tales, but there have been several instances of the kind in real life, and it is quite possible that the young and good-looking chauffeur with attractive manneis, will soon find a difficulty in obtaining a situation in a family in which there are daughters romantically inclined. Unequal Marriages.—Mr. Carnegie was reported to have said, with reference to his niece's wedding, thathe would rather see a girl married to some wealthy man no matter what his station in life, than to some "worth- less duke." The meaning of the comment is obvious, and one only needs a passing acquaintance with the "young man of the period" to know that there is a good deal of truth in it At the same time the remark is even more applicable to the other side, and speaking generally, a young lady of high birth and training can- not make a more serious mistake than that of engaging herself to a man who is greatly below her in the social scale. It is, of course, not impossible that such a marriage may prove to be a happy one, but the proba- bility is so great against such an experience that it is something like madness on the part of the lady to make such a venture. The social freedom which the modern girl enjoys has led her to take her destiny into her own hands with more assurance, but the same view may be expressed with equal confidence whenever the lady's friends, and particularly her parents, disapprove of the marriage. Lace Toilettes.—The entire lace gown will play at prominent part in the fashions of the present season, real old lace, mounted over chiffon, and then upon satin or silver tissue, ranking first of course. Closely-fitting gowns of handsome Irish lace will also be much seen, while for out-door fetes, toilettes built of Alengon lac* will be in evidence. The idea is sure to be copied ty the cheap manufacturer when the mode will have to be set aside, but at present the entire lace robe is chic in he e xtreme. Summer Games.—Croquet will be very popular this summer, and it is one of the most sociable as well as one of the most suitable games for women and girls. It is at least free from the objections sometimes urged against hockey and similar games that are injurious or undignified. Even tennis has been denounced on this score, hut though one may not hnve much sympathy with such protests. the revival of croquet is a welcome addi- tion to summer attractions. Another lawn game which is once more coming into favor is bowls, and this also is one in which women may join without any sacrifice of dignity, to which the athletic girl is supposed to attach no importance. Sculling, punting and golfing too, are generally increasing in favor. Custom in fact has happily made "women free of every sport and game," and though we have for years been hearing the same -objections to any kind of exercise for girls, the protests have fortunately been disregarded. The Vogue for Plue.—Despite the fact that blue has had a great vogue this spring, we are promised a "blue summer" all shades—from bright " royal " to a dull powder hue—being modish. The Private View at the Royal Academy furnished many examples of this particular craze. Lady Warwick who was frankly interested in the portrait of herself and her little son, wore a pastel blue cloth gown and bolero, her large black hat being trimmed with a lone shaded pastel blue ostrich feather. Lady Kitty Ogilvy, who, with her mother the Countess of Airlie, diligently studied the pictures, ass also dressed in blue of a dark royal shade a wreath of forget-me-note trimming her flat straw hat. Again one of the most striking blue toilettes—worn by a lady well-known in the theatrical profession—was of hyacinth blue taffetas, the skirt being pleated all round, and innocent of trimming, while the bodice was of the simple folded persuasion. An old-world white muslin lichu, embroidered in white and a knot of blue-mauve sweet peas, were the only relief. Concerning Trimmings.—Valenciennes lace and "broderic Anglaise " may be said to be the trimmings of the season. The latter appears upon insertions, flouncings, yoke and sleeve pieces, shirt blouses, skirts, wraps and headgear, while little in the way of under- wear or dainty toilettes is deemed complete without a touch of Valenciennes. Broad-brimmed picture hats, covered smoothly with batiste embroidered in "broderic Anglaise," the under side of the brim faced with tiny overlapping frills of Valenciennces, are in great favour in the millinery world, while tailors are embellishing the collars, cuffs, and fronts, and many of their new cloth gowns with Valenciennes lace. Ere the summer is past, we shall, in all probability have wearied of broderic Anglaise, and save for underwear, of Valen- ciennes lace. MADGE.</p>
