        <p>country. A mission like that ought to have the effect of raising people and putting them on a higher plat- form. Sunday schools and churches were like lifeboats, around which was the open sea, full of temptation, trial and difficulty. Many men, she continued, were mad after the games which passed away. Why could not they be mad after the things which lived on for ever? Great credit wasidne to the members of that mission for their perseverance and hard work, especially when it was borne in mind that they did that work without the help of a paid minister. A great deal had been said against bazaars lately, but still they were a means of helping to raise the income to carry on good work. In conclusion she would urge them to do their utmost to induce others to join the mission. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr. George Dale, seconded by Mr. David Hill, a vote of thanks was accorded to the chair- man and Mrs. Simpson. The entertainments during the sale idcluded selections by the Warrington Juvenile Harmonic Choir (conducted by Mr. T. Stough) and Mr. Vane's orchestra. At Friday's opening ceremony Mrs. Hannan effectively rendered a solo, Hr. B. Wadsworth accompanying.</p>
