      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Successful Sale of Work</p>
        <p>To have raisel £315 by their annual sale of work is an achievement upon which the workers in connection with St. Paul's Church are to be congratulated. The sale,which had for its object the support of the assistant clergy fund and parochial organizations, was held in Kendrick-street Schools on Monday and proved gratify- ingly successful. The stalls were attractively arranged, and were in charge of the following : No. I—Working party stall: Mesdames Lord, Gibson Smith, Chorlev, and Worrall, and Miss D. Reid. No. 2 stall: The Misses Geddes and Mrs. Hepherd. No. 3 stall: Mesdames Bracecamp, Lindley, J. Lawton, T. Locker, and H. Jolley. and Miss A. Walsh. No. 4 stall: Mesdames H. Collin and Burgess (Birmingham), and Miss M. Collin. No. 5 stall: The Misses Finney (2) and Fowler. No. 6 stall: Mesdames Robinson and Binks. Refreshment stall: Mesdames Beesley and Bailey, and the Misses Westwell, Smethurst (2), Houghton, Wainwright, Rogers, Pickton Ashurst and Norman. Flower stall: Mesdames John Rigby and Greenough. Bran tub: The Misses Ada Collin, Annie Burton. and Dolly McNeill. Picture postcard stall: Miss Dolly Bracecamp and Master Frank Bracecamp. There was a large attendance at the opening of the sale by Mrs. Henry Greenall. The VICAR (Rev. W. Brazecamp) in explaining the necessity for the sale, said that though they had not the , poorest congregation in Warrington, St. Paul's Church ' was theyoorest in the sense that it had only a small endowment. They got no grant from any society towards the clergy fund,and with the exception of £l2B, they had to dbtain all they raised from three sources. First of all, there were the collections in the church,and very willingly and readily the people gave, showing that they were anxious to help themselves. Then they had outside friends, who gave subscriptions from time to time,and the annual sale, the proceeds of which were given to the additional curates fund and various parochial organizations. He was sure that those present would do their best to help them. They tried as a congregation to help themselves, and they felt encouraged when other kind friends helped them. (Hear, hear). It was not necessary for him to introduce the lady who had come to open the sale of work, as she was well known in Warrington and the dis- trict. They gladly welcomed Mrs. Greenall, and appre- ciated her kindness; they were, in fact, deeply grateful to all who had done anything to make the sale a success. (Applause). Mrs. HENRY GREENALL in a short speech remarked that the sale was the means of doing a great deal of good every year, and it was worthy of their best sup- port. The Vicar had told them the objects in view, and all she had to do was to ask all present to help the ladies, who had worked so hard to furnish the stalls, to clear them of their contents. She hoped that before they separated that evening everything would be sold, and that the proceeds would be large enough to please even the Vicar. (Laughter and applause.) Mr. J. H. SmETninisT (the vicar's warden), in pro- posing a vote of thanks to Mrs. Greenall for her kind- ness in opening the sale, said he thought it would be out of place if he did not mention the fact that he practically stood in the shoes of amen who has recog- nized almost as the father of their congregation, the late Alderman Sutton, who last year had something to say to them. He felt that the name which Mrs. Greenall had the honour to bear, was a name to conjure with in Warrington, and he felt qertain that, as in the past, they had achieved great success with a Greenall opening their sale, so when it finished that evening they would still be in the proud position of having had a most successful gathering. (Applause). Mr. joHN RIGBY seconded, and the motion was carried with acclammation. The VICAR announced that in the future the sale of -work would be held on Easter Thursday owing to the difficulty experienced in obtaining the schools just after the Easter holidays. He had to apologize for the absence of Lady Greenall, but they would be interested to know that she had sent them a splendid nest egg. (Applause). The proceeds for the day:realized £315.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Helsby Water Supply.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present: Messrs. G. Pilling (presiding),T. Baxter, T. Wright, J. Stokes, C. E. Linaker, J. Douglas, J. Hallows,C.Roylance,J. Spruce, J. Whitley,N. Collins, S. Egerton-Warburton, J. L. Jones, J. Smith, J. Bolshaw, J. B. Chadwick, T. Antwis,T. Guest, H.lMawdslay,J. H. DIMS% H. M. Davies, H. Eyes,?. Mounfield,A. Broome, W. Harrison. E. Bretherton, R. E. Chrimes, .T. Fryer, J. Rutt,r,R. Billington.G. F.Ashton (clork),W. E. Hough, (assistant clerk). J. Farrington (inspector), J. Swinton (surveyor), and Dr. Adams (medical officer). FRODSTIAM SEWAGE. The Clerk reported the receipt of a letter from Mr. Hunter, on behalf of the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany, enclosing for the confirmation by the Council a plan and section showing the proposed diversion of the drain through which the sewage of Frodsham was at present passing. The diversion would be effected by means of al5 inch main to join the diversion of the Holpool gutter and syphon in course of construction. Mr. Ashton further reported that at a meeting of the Frodsham Sewerage Committee it had been resolved that Mr. Diggle, or failing him, Messrs. Below and Priest,bs asked to advise the Council upon the proposal of the Ship Canal Company. The action of the Frodsham Sewerage Committee was confirmed.</p>
