      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MAY 6th. 1905.</p>
        <p>Ministers have re- PARLIAMENT AND sumed their Parlia- THE ALIEN. mentary labours, as</p>
        <p>their friends assure us, in a very cheerful frame of mind. Everything unpleasant that happened before Easter they have forgotten, and remember only as a nightmare. The party has made up its differences, and is man- fully resolved to go through with the Government programme of legislation, such as it is. Not much is said now about the Unemployed Bill. Mr. BAtroun remarked on Tuesday that he could not say when the second reading would come on, but there is a new zeal for the Aliens Bill, which, in the form it took last session, interested its authors so little that they made no great efforts te carry it. When the Bill was re-introduced this year, there was no signs of enthusiasm in the Ministerial benches, but now the tune is changed. Eight Liberal r3presentatives of the East End of London—two members and six candidates—have issued a declaration in favour of the Bill, and,with its original sponsors and guardians, have come to the conclusion that there must be something in it. We do not understand that Mr. BUXTON or his East End colleagues support the Bill in its entirety. Very few people are opposed to it in its entirety, and most Liberals have always held that measures should be taken for the expulsion of foreign criminals and for putting an embargo on their immigration into this country. In moving his amendment on Tuesday Sir CHARLES DILKE com- plained tbat there was no sufficient guarantee in the Bill for the asylum hitherto granted to the victims of political or religious persecution. The Jewish Board of Guardians in the East End make the same complaint, although they are in favour of restricting the influx of aliens who belong to the undesired class. The Bill will become law, we pre- sume, but not until it has been severely over- hauled in Committee. It will then be interesting to see whether the limitation of its agency to eight ports will make the new law effectual. We have already pointed out that the most drastic proviso will penalise the shipowners who bring in the for- bidden alien. They will have to take him back at their expense if he be condemned at the port of debarkation. Taking him back will not be easy,and it is possible that the shipper will find this branch of the business not worth pursuing. It is also possible that the regulation will prove to be unworkable. Aliens of the least desirable variety may continue to arrive in spite of this, smuggled in at ports where there is little or no official super- vision. The Bill may yet disappoint the hopes of its projectors. On the other hand, we are bound to say that the alternative legislation suggested by Sir CHARLES DILKE is :rather impracticable. He wants to see sweating prohibited in the East End. This is part of a vast economic problem, by no means limited to a particular district, and if Sir CHARLES is going to prohibit sweating, he must lay down the principle that prices shall not be determined by competition—a very far-reaching principle indeed, If Sir CHARLES DILKE thinks that the next Government or some later Govern- ment will legislate on that principle, we must confess that we are not so sanguine.</p>
        <p>The fact that it has been quoted in a newspaper seems to have inflated that insiguificant local publication called the "Warrington Review " to bursting point. We thought we bad honoured it when we reprinted two letters from its columns last week because they were penned by the pros • pective candidates at the next election and afforded an interesting contrast in style. " Alas ! the gratitude of men '• " it hath left us mourning! We are charged with trying" to make something out of these letters" and our right to complain about "anyone's subscription" is questioned. The hard words " presumptuous," cheek," and " impudence " are hurled at our devoted head, but with so clumsy a hand that they totally miss their objective. We carefully re- frained from criticising ,Mr. Pierpoint's or " any- one's subscription, " as the incautious writer in the "Review "may ascertain by the exercise of average faculties. There is an unmistakable Con- trast in the style of the two letters, and that is all we called attention to, and if the "Review " feared a comparison being made it should not have been so lamentably indiscreet as to publish them together. Evidently somebody's knuckles have been severely rapped,and the pain caused thereby has produced a vitupera- tive explosion. We are sorry for our small con- temporary, but the lesson has to be learnt that the smallest of slips has often the most painful consequences.</p>
        <p>- The Aldermen and- Councillors of Warrington were in a most amicable frame of mind on Tues- day, when they met for the monthly Council meeting. Instead of a three hours discussion,only nineteen minutes were required to consider the Mayor's communications, the business of fifteen committees, and two special motions. This is not a record, but it was a relief. Once in a way a short meeting is appreciated, especially by the Mayor and the press. Both have an arduous task when the Council indulges in wordy warfare at every opportunity.</p>
        <p>There was one discussion of a decidedly inter- esting character. It was only to be expected that there would be differences of opinion as to the expediency of adopting the Library. Sub Com- mittee's resolution in regard to opening negotia- tions with Mr. Andrew Carnegie, but few people expectod the denouement brought about by Councillor Wright. The Mayor had already expressed his strong distaste for "cadging," when Councillor Wright rose and moved that the resolu- tion be referred back. His objections were more personal than those of the Mayor. In his view Mr. Carnegie is anything but a philanthropist, and I the Council was rather surprised when Councillor Wright recalled the Isistory of the strike at Mr. Carnegies's Homestead works, Pittsburg in 1891, and charged the millionaire with oppression of his workpeople. Mr. CarnegiEi's money was " not clean enough for a town liker•Warrington " said the Labour mpresentative with considerable emphasis. This is a new view of the Carnegie donations to urban and municipal authorities, and opens up a nice question in ethics. We frequently hear of huge bequests to religious andphilauthropic bodies by wealthy members of the brewing trade,but very rarely of a refusal to accept the money, which has incontestably been wrung from the poor at the sacrifice of health, happiness and morality. The Carnegie millions have not been acquired by methods free from reproach and we are glad to note that the voice of conscience is being raised, Mr. Carnegie. has been busily engaged in a course of restitution for a dozen years past, but he has not yet bought the good-will of the dernonacy, which looks askance at the millionaire and his money. We have much sympathy with the Socialistic view that the accumulation of vast wealth is immoral, and Councillor Wright deserves praise for his courageous outspokeness.</p>
        <p>Public morality is a subject which Warrington people have had opportunities of considering in various lights and on which they have expressed firm opinions. It is now considered wrong in Warrington for a representative of the. ritepayers on a public body to profit by that position even if his conduct be perfectly legal. Other muni- cipalities have not advanced quite so far along the path of public probity but every now and then there are indications of an awakening conscience in ',this direction. A remarkable circumstance has come to light in Crewe this week which should serve as an object lesson for Warrington and for the country. Dis- closures were made which provbd that a member of the Council and an ex-Mayor had profited by an agreement between three printers in the borough to pay each other csrtain percentages in the event of any of them securing the Corporation contracts for printing and stationery. Though the matter was eight or nine years old the Crewe Town Council adopted a resolution requesting the delinquent councillor to refund the money, and this course the latter meekly agreed to. The argument that it was unfair to rake up such a thing after the lapse of so long a time is amusing. We know of no process of evolution by which wrong becomes right. Time obliterates and changes many things, but it does not absolve the evil-doer from ultimate judgment. Ou this point all systems of ethics would, we opine, be in com- plete unity.</p>
        <p>The provision of meals for under-fed school chil- dren is henceforth to be undertaken by Boards of Guardians working in conjunction with the education authorities. But the meals are not to be absolutely" free" for, except in cases where special circumstances warrant an exemption, the cost will be recoverable from the parents. On the face of it, we are informed, the Local Govern- ment Board's Order would appear to meet the local difficulties. Probably the principal point of objection will be the disfranchisement of the parents of children receiving relief. As far as Warrington is concerned, the informa- tion in possession of those who are brought into touch with the working of the schools points to the fact that comparatively few children are sent to school under-fed, through the inability of the parents to provide for them. Of course, there are exceptional cases of honest, deserving people who ought to receive help. Tne difficulty, however, is to get such inhabitants to make known their wants, In Warrington, it is stated, the injudicious feed ing of children is a great fault, whilst others go without sufficient food largely owing to the neglect of the parents. The Town Council, it will be remembered, recently appointed a committee to go into the question, but no further action has been taken. The Education Authority has not vet resolved upon any steps to put the Local Government Board order into effect.</p>
        <p>To the many societies which Warrington har- bours has now been added a branch of the Anti- Vivisection Society and on Tuesday members and friends listened with interest to an address by Miss Lind, a Swedish lady who has won some fame for her endeavours on behalt of the brute creation. Like the anti-vaccinationists and passive resisters, the anti-vivisectionists seem to be increasing in number. To us there appears a resemblance between vivi- section and vaccination which should not be lost sight of in considering the question. The one consists very largely of inoculation and the other wholly se. It is a generally accepted theory that vaccination is a minimiser and even preventive of small-pox, and the legislature has made it com- pulsory. The value of vaccination is obviously a strong argument in favour of vivisection, for if by inoculation the cure or prevention of other diseases may perchance be discoverable then the end justifies the means. Miss Lind, if consistent. should be an anti-vaccinationist as well as an anti- vivisectionist. That steps are necessary to prevent brutality in the name of science we admit, but wa think the balance of argument is heavily against the entire abolitioh of vivisection.</p>
        <p>St. Austin's Ward vacancy is to be contested after all, though the successful candidate will only be Pntitled to sit until the November elections. Dr. Joseph and Mr. E. Horrocks are the gentlemen nominated, and both have issued addresses to the electors. Being in marked contrast to one another, the two addresses form an interesting study. It voters are really much influenced by the candi- dates' profession of faith we should imagine</p>
        <p>they will quickly decide which gentleman to support. Dr. Joseph is en economist while Mr. Horrocks seems to be filled with the idea of con- verting the Borough at once into a modern Utopia. Everything "free "—breakfasts, baths, edlcation, libraries &amp;c.—is the burden of his cry. The Libour candidate's ideals are excellent, but he appear to have overlooked the imporant fact that to attempt half of them would send the rates up with a rush. Money must he saved before it can be spent, and we should imagine that the heavily-burdened ratepayers will plump solidly for economy.</p>
        <p>Lord Kitchener's appeal for funds for the relief of the sufferers by the recent earthquake in India is likely to touch the British public more nearly than most appeals of the kind de. Human sympathy is never lacking in England.as the many successful Mansion House Funds for the victims of plague and famine in India, or of earthquake or disaster in other p4rts of the world connected or otherwise with the British Empire, have proved. But there is in this case a special reason why funds shuuld be forthcoming readily. The principal sufferers by the earthquake were the Goorkhas,who formed the garrison of Dharrnsala, and Euglishmec have for many a long day looked with a pride upon the hardy little race,which has furnished us with some of our most reliable soldiers in the Indian Empire. In the Mutiny their faithfulness was one of the brightest incidents in a black page in British-Indian history. Side by side with the British soldier they have fought gallantly in many a frontier cam paiti, and their loyalty to their officers and to th 3 flag has been attested over and over again by many a gallant pioneer or upholder of British rule. We have dwelt upoi. their deeds and their faithfulness with proad certainty that the test, should it come, would find them ready to do again what they have done in the past. For this reason, English people- should hasten to respond to the call Lord Kitchner has issued. Every soldier will feel that we are not doing our duty unless the money that is so urgently required is subscribed readily and generously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>" Are we down-hearted ? " was the cry of both Hull and Warrington partizans at Leeds prior to the match. Afterwards, the cup-winners had it all to themselves, and the responsive No-o-o !:vas particularly emphatic. Tuesday's Council meeting while not the shortest, constituted a record in another respect. It was the first meeting Alderman Tinnion has ' attended at which he has not spoken. Even thb minutes of his Committee were proposed by another member, Councillor Roberts, the chairman not then having arrived.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. A. H. Crosfield and two or three Warrington Aldermen saw Warrington win the cup. We hear they waxed as enthusiastic as any typical "wirepuller." There is some tale, too, about Mr. Crosfield's hat-band. It did not come back with the hat if we are rightly informed. Well! we'll all risk a hat-band when Mr. Crosfield wins the Parliamentary stakes. * * * A fine plot of land off Hood-lane has been secured by Messrs. Crosfield's workpeople for their big recrea- tion scheme. It has already been enclosed, and the work of laying it out for the various sports will shortly be commenced. There should be pleasant times in store for the soap-workers, who are to be congratulated upon this happy innovation. * 5 * j A cartoon in one of the daily papers this week hits !off capitally the point of view of the lazy parent who now learns that he is to be made to pay for the "free' breakfasts to school-children. We really believe some people think that the State ought to relieve them of all parental responsibility. A man and woman's duty in life is not exactly completed by the bringing of children into the world.</p>
        <p>* There was quite an imposing array of Town Councillors and " Heathens to take part in a trial rim over the famous Light Railway No. 1 at Stockton Heath. Everything was in readiness, apparently, when a steamer thoughtlessly passed along the Canal and disturbed the Swing Bridge. Somehow or other the expensive new equipment did not work smoothly and the car could not cross. The little procession, therefore, did a right-about turn</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. Andrew Carnegie, the man of many millions states in his latest work:—" Not from palace or castle, but from the cottage have come, or can come, the needed leaders of our race, under whose guidance it is to ascend." Providence has been kind to the poor, but the rich have robbed many of their heritage. The grinding, grasping systems which obtain to-day ore means of speculating in the brains and bodies of the poor, and many would-be leaders are sacrificed on the altar of surplus wealth and sacred trusts. A shorthand writer is to be engaged to note the proceedings of the Committee appointed to investigate the £lOO scandal and "any other matter affecting the honour or integrity of the members of the Council.' IWe should say he is in for a busy time. The cost of I the report will, we presume, have to be paid for by' r some of the Councillors who are on the Committee. At any rate that was the course adopted by the Council in regard to the last inquiry, when the Non-Traders were requested to report to the Mayor and left to pay the bill !</p>
        <p>* * * ' There is much talk in London of another by-election to fill the vacancy in Mr. 13alfou1.'8 ministry which the Brighton electors refused to allow Mr.Loder to occupy. Major Evans Gordon is mentioned as the new candi- date for office, ;so the Government is evidently pre- pared to try its luck in the Stepney Division. If an seat may reasonably be consideredy' safe "we slithink that Stepney is the one. Anyhow, we hope Major Gordon will get his appointment and thus give Mr. Durham Stokes, the Liberal-Labour Candidate for the division, the opportunity of emulating Mr. Villiers, the hero of Brighton. dird</p>
        <p>* * * , Heroism had its fitting recognition on Thursday, when the men who risked their lives to save othera in the recent sewer fatality, received the Royal Humane Society's rewards. Though the ceremonY itself was quiet, all Warrington may be said to have endorsed tlds rendering of " Honour to whom honour is due." Coroner Brighouse paid a glowing, tribute to John Stockton's bravery when he said it equalled anY of the deeds on the battle-field or on the sea, of which we read with pride. This distressing fatality has shown that there is true English heroism ir! the humbler walks of life.</p>
        <p>* * There has been quite * a startling discovery at Stockton Heath this week. While eating an egg, Mr, F. Harrington, of Glenroy, Walton New-road, Was surprised o find a sixpenny piece embedded in the yolk. The egg, it is stated, was one of a dozen pur- chased at the shop of a well-known Parish Councillor. Some people are asking whether the shop-keeper would be right in demanding the return of the coin. Certainly the purchaser received more than hi 4 money's-worth. Mr. Harrington regards the find as so unique that the coin is to be suspended from hie watch-guard. * * *</p>
        <p>The municipal bowling green at Victoria Park is looking very green and , healthy, and it is satisfactorY to learn from the Mayor that it will 800/1 be ready for play. What sort of patronage it receives will depenn, very largely on the amount of the charges, anti. whether the publicity of its situation renders the grew unpopular. In this latter respect we consider the green a perpetual monument to someone's folly. Had it, been taken MO yards further into the Park itg attractiveness to the average bowler would have teen considerably enhanced. At present it is easy tn imagine a crowd of inexpert idlers leaning over the fence and making caustic remarks about the novice's inability to reach the "Jack." This sort of thing won't encourage the novice.</p>
        <p>* * What is it that is going to be "a boon and a blessing to (wo)men ? " Not "the Pickwick, the Owl, and the' Waverley p.en," but a sewing-mashine in which the shuttle is dispensed with, and does the sewing direct from two reels of cotton. The inventor is Mr. Dennis Flannagan, of Clayton-le-Moors, near Blackburn, and before very lon6 the machine will be on the market., ! The thought of it creates visions of endless sewing wit. 4 out let or hindrance ,and to dressmakers tailors, Stc., must mean a great saving of time. To "go one better,'. supposing it were possible to insert one of the reels of. a mile of cotton, and with another mile in the usual Place, one could almost, like Tennyson's brook, "go on for ever."</p>
        <p>\</p>
        <p>i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>Liberal Women's Delegates. The local delegates to the Women's Liberal Federation meetings in London in June will be MN. E. Osborne and Mrs. Simpson. "PREMIER" CYCLES at 9 guineas for Best Grade. unsurpassed.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Marriage of Miss Parr. A marriage has been arranged between Robert Soam3 Jocelyn, only son of Lieutenant-Colonel the Hon. Robert Jocelyn, brother and heir-presumtive to the Earl of Roden, and Elinor Jessie, second daughter of Mr. J. Charlton Parr, Grappenhall Heyes, and Staunton Park, Staunton-on-Arrow. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LeNcAswtnic.—Advt. H. K AswroN's LIFE Tomo is a help for your health.—Advt. Local Slander Case. One of the cases down for hearing at the Manchester Assizes; on Thursday, before Mr. Justice Walton and a special jury, was an action for slander brought by Reginald Herbert Henry Birtles, auctioneer, Warring- ton, ngainst Thomas Heatley, farmer, Marton Bank, Winsford,—Mr. E. Acton (instructed by Messrs Arthur Browne and Co.), repreiented the plaintiff, and Mr. Shea, K.C. and Mr. Spencer Hogg (instructed by Mr. J. H. Cook, Winsford), appeared for the defen- dant. After the luncheon adjournment, Mr. Shea announced that a satisfactory settlement between the parties had been arrived at. Defendant's view was that he had never uttered anything to the discredit of the plaintiff. He certainly did not intend to do so, and there was no imputation whatever against the plaintiff's character.—No mention was made in Court of the amount paid in settlement. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH•CLASS 1111:NtutAL WA.vms.—Advt. Oddfellows' Quarterly Meeting. The quarterly meeting of the \Varrington District of. the National Independent Order of Oddiellows was held on Saturday. all the lodges being represented. Grand Master William Peanington presided. P.G. Thomas Gregson was sleeted Banner Master, and the District Secretary was elected to represent the district at the Annual Movable Committee to be held in Whit- week at Bridlington Quay. P.P.G.M. Walter Duncan presented the late Grand Master (Bro. George Hands) with a P.P.G.M. jewel on behalf of the district. Bro. Duncan said he considered Bro. Hands had been a good officer, and had carried out his duties very satis- factorily. P.P.G.M. Thomas Knight and the District Secretary also bore testimony to Bro. Hands' work. The District Secretary said he was pleased to say that Bro. Hands was now the treasurer to the "Loyal Perseverance Lodge.—Bro. Hands thanked Bro. Duncan and the district for the present. He said he was at first afraid of going through the offices, and had , he not recerved the help he bad he would have failed. He thanked them all, and especially the District Secretary. He would still have the seine interest in the welfare of the district. Owing to the high price of Hogs, Bacon has advanced 3d. to Id. per lb.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S REGIS-CLASS MINERAL WATsns.—Advt., New Hall Opened at Stockton Heath. The commodious premises at Victoria-square, Stockton Heath, which have recently been erected by Mr. Peers Egerton-Warburton, were opened OR Satur- day, when a large number of residents in the district attended and partook of afternoon tea at the invita- tion of Mr. Warburton. There is no doubt that the building is a decided ornament to the village. The front which Laces on- London-road and Grappenall-road is of terracotta brick work with stone facings. On the Grappenhall-road side there is a fine arcade with two shops. An excellent stone staircase leads to a can in the interior of the building and s large hall in which political meetings, dances, &amp;c. will be held. Bedrooms have been provided far boarders which, though small, are well furnished and welllighted. Recreation will be provided in the shape of draughts &amp;c., and accommodation has been made for a billiard table. Every convenience is provided and there is 'little doubt that the building will be largely patronized. Tho work reflects the greatest credit upon the builder (Mr. C. W. Davenport), and the architect (Mr. Kirby, Liverpool).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>POTATO MARKET AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>THE POTATO MARKET AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Farmers and the Question.</p>
        <p>A meeting of farmers and others interested in the potato market at Stockton Heath MIS held in the Stretton schools on Thursday evening, whenafter a dis- cussion lasting for an hour and a half it was decided to continue the market, and a committee of twelve was appointed to try to procure a plot of land on which to " shoot " potatoes. Farmers from the surrounding dis- trict were present in large numbers, and Mr. Billington, a prominent mover in the arranging of the meeting, was voted to the chair. At the outset, Mr. Billington read a letter from Mr. Roger C. Parr, expressing the hope that some arrangement would 1:13 come to He had neard there was a chance of the market being held in some field and he hoped that was so, as when the trains got into running he did not think there would be room for the market in Victoria-301re. Mr. Billington pro• ceeded to emphasise the necessity of looking at the question from different standpoints. He pointed out that the rapid growth of Stockton Heath had removed the pieces of waste land at the sides of the roadway with the result that for the past few years they had experienced great difficulty in carrying on the market without causing an obstruction. Moreover complaints had been received from the residents in the district about a nuisance being caused by the leaving of potato tops in the roadway. He thought the time had arrived when they should take some steps to prevent the obstruction and nuisance. Mr. Billington proceeded to illustrate the lack of railway facilities in the district. The potatoes had, he said, to he conveyed either to the Central Station or to the Bridgewater Canal Wharf at London Bridge. He did not think the stopping of the Market wyuld do away with the abstraction, as in that event the potatoes would have to be taken to the London Bridge Wharf and there loaded on to flats, and the obstruction would be as great as ever. Whst they I r hese(7iujenaotpti tcine of land or required which to t do wld as suti oe tam ka reksette steps t' would be any difficulty in that direction. Mr. C. T. Whitley asked whether the road authorities had any right to stop them holding the market ? and whether the Warrington Corporation had a right to interfere? The Chairman replied that the road authorities had no right to prevent them using the road for a certain length of time provided they did not cause an obstruc- tion. They had as much right to use the road as any- one else, provided they only stood for a reasonable length of time. He did not think the Corporation had any right to interfere so long as they were outside the borough. In answer to Mi. Barber the chairman said farmers had not been summoned for obstructing the road. Buyers had been summoned but had not been fined. Mr. John Darbyshire in a lengthy speech advocated the changing of the name from that of a potato market to one of a potato exchange. He stoutly denied that the farmers obstructed the roadway, and pointed oat I that greater obstructions were caused by markets in Manchester and Warrington. The reason for the outcry against the potato market was, he said, that Stockton Heath was a comparatively new place and objected to the farmers going there in order to show their authority and he would advise the Stockton Heath people not to overrate their powers and be guilty of an absurdity in trying to "snuff " out a useful and legitimate trade. Mr. Derby- shire was proceEdine to deal with questions wide of the issue before the meeting when he was called to order by the Chairman and resumed his seat. Mr. Eyes said he had been informed by a member of the Stocktun Heath Parish Council that the Council I were quite in favour of the market being held at Stockton Heath. Mr. Baxter advised the securing of a piece of land above London Bridge for the holding of the market. Mr. Daxbyshire questioned whether the Corporation markets inspectors were not trespassing when they went to the market, and was reminded that the inspectors had a perfect right to be on the highway. Mr. Derbyshire: But they have no right to pry into our business. Next time they come tell them to go home. Mr. T. Ditchfleld argued that the farmers had a per- fect right to use the roadway as a market. The obstruction he pointed out was caused by the "shoot- ing "°f potatoes and be moved that a piece of land be obtained on which th, "shooting" could be done. Mr. Williamson advocated the petitioning of the County Council; to discourage any interference on the part of the police. At this stage the business of the meeting got a little mixed and to straighten matters the Chairman asked those present to vote as to whether they "were in favour of the continuance of the market, the reply being unanimously in the affirmative. Mr. Purslow thought the County Council were com- pelled tofprovide a permanent market and thought noth- ing should be done until the County Council moved in the matter. The Chairman pointed out that the County Council had not the power to establish a market. Upon a vote beint taken Mr. Ditchtleld's proposition was carried and Messrs. Billington, John Whitlow, D. Percival, G. Egerton Warburton, Ditchtleld, J. Rutter, H.Eyes,j. Bolshaw, E. Bretherton, J. Whitley, T. Baxter, and W. Turner, were appointed a committee to go on with the question of obtaining the land for shooting purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Examinet, Saturday, May 6, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examinet, Saturday, May 6, 1905.</p>
        <p>UNDER-FED SCHOOL CHILDREN. Meals to , be Supplied by Guardians.</p>
        <p>COST RECOVERABLE FROM PARENTS</p>
        <p>For lome considerable time, public opinion has been agitated on the question of whether public authorities should provide meals for under-fed school children. Considerable interest therefore centres in an Order of the Local Government Board dealing with the subject. Broadly stated, the effect will be that meals can be supplied to under-fed school children by Boards of Guardians at the instance of the officers of the education authority, managers and teachers at the schools, and the cost will be recoverable from the parents unless a special exemption from payment is agreed to by the Local Government Board. The order takes the form of a circular to Guardians explaining the powers and duties of Barth of Guardians in re- lation to under-fed children at public elementary schools. The doeument deals only with cases in which special application is made either to the Guardians or their officer for the allowance of relief to a child under sixteen in attenda ce at an elementary school. If an application is tof sudden and urgent necessity the Guardians ust inquire whether it is rendered necessary by tho habitual neglect of the father to pro- vide adequate food. If the Guardians are satisfied that such is the case any relief is to be regarded as a loan. If the Guardians are not so satisfied, the relief May be given as a loan or be "considered as so given." Before granting a loan the Guardians are to notify the parent in order to give him an opportunity of provid- ing the food, and thus avoiding disfranchisement. Relief is only to be given for one month on each application. In cases of urgency the parent must be notified as soon as possible that relief has been given. The Guardians are to take proceedings for the recovery of any relief except in cases where they report to the Local Government Board and obtain that authority's sanction to abstain from pi oceedings. In a circular to the Education authorities, the Local Government Board point out that a child may be sent to school underfed because its parents are permanently impoverished, because of temporary illness 9r loss of employment which for a time incapacitates the parentor through neglect of the parent to make proper provision. The Board suggest that in the second class of cases (temporary disability)volunta.ry agencies will probably be able to do what is neceasary. The first and third classes (permanent poverty and neglect) are cases for special application to the Guardians for relief. With regard to the latter cases, it is pointed out that the Prevention of Cruelty to Children Act (1604) and the Vagrancy Act (1824) enable the Guardians to take proceedings against the Lather for neglect. Special applications have to be made to the Guardians or the relieving officers by managers of schools, by a teacher if empowered by the managers to do so, or by an officer appointed lov the local education authority. Of course, ,under the ordinary rules of relief the Guardians and their officers are bound to give relief in cases of urgent necessity if application is made by the child on any responsible person.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON' GUARDIANS AND THE QUESTION. _</p>
        <p>MR. JOLLEY AND PARENTAL RESPONSIBILITY. There was some discussion on the subject of free meals for underfed school children at the minting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday. A letter was received from Mr. E. Lees, secretary of the Warrington Branch of the Independent Labour Party, forwarding a copy of Mr. Arthur Henderson's Bill which seeks to grant powers to local authorities to provide meals for school children. He trusted that it would meet with the appreciation of the Board. The CLIIRK (Mr. J. C. Sutton) said he would like to call attention to the fact that the subject had been dealt with by the Government in the f rin of an Order. It seemed to him that a serious amount of work sv. Id arise ont of it, and the charge was a very serious ques- tion. He suggested the appointment of a committee consiftinglof the relieving °facers, collector,and several Guardians to consider the matter. The theory of the Order was that children who were not getting fed as they ought to be, were to be fed at the expense of the Guardians in some way, and the Guardians were to sue the parents to recover the cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>A prophet is, for once, "honoured in his own country." My prophesy last week that Warrington would win the Northern Union Cup has been fulfilled, mid I may now pose as a seer of the first magnitude. The story of how the cup was captured is told in another column, but there still remain a few things to say.</p>
        <p>To everyone's surprise Warrington were easy I winners. The fact that the score was only 0-0 as ! nothing to go by. Had it been necessary to double ' the points Warrington could have done so, for the Hull men were beaten fore and aft in the second portion of the game. It was only in the first half, with the wind and sun in their favour, that the Rovers made any sort of show. Even then Warrington were I quite as prominent on the attack and appeared to have I a little in hand. After the first five minutes the con- fidence of the Warrington supporters appeared likely to be justified. The only fear, prior to the match,had ' , been that the Rovers' forwards might obtain c )mmand ! of the ball on the heavy ground, and it was soon seen j that the Warrington eight were capable of holding their own. There was no evidence of the Rovers' I alleged advantage in weight, and before the close the • forwards as well as the backs had to play second fiddle to the active Warringtonians. • I lam quite sure that the Rdvers were not at their ; best. The strain of their first final evidently told up3n , the players, and there was a marked difference in the , cool and calculating play of Warrington. Probably, lif the Hull men had had the good fortune to score I before half time—and they were very near doing so. I once—they would have given their opponents a very ! hard fight, but the Warrington defence was superb, and there was a very pleased smile on the 'faces of , Hallam and his men when the interval'arrived.</p>
        <p>IThe resumption of hostilities soon settled the fate of ; the Rovers. Warrington went off with splendid dash land Fish was a scorer within three minutes of the start. He received a pass from Isherwood when there Iwas apparently no opening, but by a great effort he got free from the clutches of West and Phipps and ' ; grounded the ball near the corner. Then one heard the famous" Wilderspool whisper!" Ten minutes later I came the incident of the match. Brooks got clear i away in his own half and with Fish in attendance the , issue of the movement was never in doubt. The young half back transferred the ball at the correct moment j for Fish to run behind the posts. It was a beautiful piece of, play and was characteristic of the Warring- ton men at their best. Hallam didn't kick a goal, but the match was over. Hull could hardly raise a gallop towards the close, and the fact that Warrington simply toyed with them explains the fact that there was no more scoring.</p>
        <p>Nearly every paper gave the credit for Fish's open- ing to Isherwood, and the mistake can readily be understood, for he and Brookes are miteli alike. The centre, however, never touched the 'ball, and the honours go deservedly to the youngest member of the team. Brooks is only 21, and beats Kenyon by a couple of months for the post of :‘ baby.' , Bravo, Brooks!</p>
        <p>- - Every man on the Warrington side played well, and there need be no fears for their future behaviour in Cup-finals—if they get there. There was a church in the distance and the tall spire may have reminded them of St. James'. At any rate they could have done no better at IVilderspool. Preston was much missed in the goal-kicking line, and had he been present he would almost certainly have bagged a trio Of 2-pointers. Prober)ly the men who distinguished themselves most were Hallam, Brooks, Fish, and Harmer. The reserve forward was ever in die van, with Boardman, Shugars and Thomas likewise conspicuous. Really, however, a good word could be said for every player on the Warrington side. The Rovers were best represented at half-back, where Barry did excellent service. At threequarter the Roi,ers were badly beaten, the Warrington quartette keeping them well in hand throughout the afternoon. Sinclair played a sound game at full back, though he did not show the expertnesaM .Hallam in getting his side out of a difficulty. Orthe forwards —who showed none of the bulk suggested by their published weights—Starks and Spackman were easily the best. The pick of the threequarters was young West, who, against a less remarkable player than Fish, would probably have accomplisherl samethlng noteworthy. Robinson, the alleged star centre, was the xvdrst man of the bunch and frequently fell a victim to Isherwood's smart tackling, while he never once succeeded in getting Xfadely going.</p>
        <p>Mr.ASHCROFT: When they are able to pay it. The CLERK said it was hot left to the Board to con- sider whether parents could pay or not. They must give a reason to the Local Government Board and app'y for their sanction to not taking proceedings. That seemed to him to be a matter which would involve a very considerable amount of correspondence under difficult conditions. The subject was one that needed the most careful consideration. Ithadatteibted a great amount of attention in the country and there was no doubt it would come before the Association of Poor. Law Unions in a broad way at the annual meeting. Meanwhile the Board had to give effect to the Order, supposing that they had applications of that kind. Mr. HICKMAN : The County Court Judge will tell you whether a parent is able to pay or not. The CLERK said it seemed to him that if the Board paid Gd. or a shilling to provide a child with food, to sue a parent for it they must necessarily spend five or six times that amount. He had seen it suggested that the best way of meeting theipresent state of affairs would be to have a generous supply of milk available for the children, without any other form of breakfast or food. If that could be done in a simple way it would save a greet number of round-about methods. Mrs. WILKINSON : What provision does the Order make for the children of a widow? The CLERK : They get relief in the ordinary way. Mrs. Wthicelsoe : If a child hes relief given, not on loan. will the rather be disfranchised ? The Ceenx. : I think he will. Continuing, the Clerk said he had forwarded a copy of the Order to Mr. A. J. Jolley, who took a very active pert in the administra- tion of relief. If Mr. Jolley had read it he (the Clerk) , ould like him to say a few words to the Board, as he ; The Leeds and Hull papers all gave Warrington was more accustomed to deal with the Warrington ' credit for a meritorious win. "Old Eher " in the relief cams than any other member of the Board. "Yorkshire Evening Post" says: "There was no Mr. JOLLEY remarked that as he had not had much I time to consider the Order, any remarks he now made Otting away from the fact that Warrington had were liable to revision. He was disposed to think it played the better-clasa football, and deservedly won was not perhaps, expedient for the Board to take any the cup, at the third time of asking. The Rovers , active steps at this stager but rather to wait till they have struggled hard and pluckily, but there was a bit saw what the bloat education authorities affecting the better class on the other side. As I have already said, Union areas did. Those authorities vvsre the County the Education Committee and the Warrington Education movement that led to Fish's second try 'was Committee. He knew the Local Government Board brilliant enough in itself to win any team a match of had sent an Order to the secretary of each Education this streauouS kind." Authority. Joint action was very desirable, and he -- apprehended that the Guardians were desirous of work_ I In the "Leeds Mercury," the football specialist ing as much as possible with the Education authorities, " Flanenr " says.: —" To pat the matter in a nutshell, because unless the question was dealt with in a large the Revere, beaten forward, had fewer chances behind, and broad spirit, it was going to open the door to a and the Warrington hicks were very smart in taking great deal of abuse, and it would do an enormous advantaee of the oppertimitieg that came their way. - amount of harm. He ventured to think personally that so the Northern Union Can final of 1905 ended in a a great deal of the cry raised in respect of under-fed children was greatly exaggrinated. He felt very strongly well-deserved victory for Warrington." , that the responsibility for the welfare of children rested with the parents, and anything that might be done that; "The Preelal "in the "Hull Daily News" says:— was likely to imperil in the smallest degree the sensi "The Warrington men adapted thetnselves more of parental responsibility was a very dangerous courageously and more effectively to the conditions thing indeed. The Order, ;as far as he than did the Rovers. l was one of the Robins' could grasp its purport, was not intended to diminish off-ua' ye, and they were deservedly beaten by a team the discretion the Guardians had in the granting of relief. When they gave relief under the new Order they greatsuperior on the day than the which vias a g deal ' would be bound to make an attempt to recover the cost score of six points to nil indicates." ' incurred, and they were likely to be involved in some , oases in considerable expense. Before granting the The same writer has also something very interest- relief, the Guardians were bound to give notice to the ing to say of the Warrington forwards. He adds "A. father of the child that they proposed to giant it, inL - fortnight ago .. . . I stated that the Warrington order to afford the father an opportunity of supplying forwards were very wiry and could heel out, more the necessary food, so that he would not run the risk of being disfranchised or of being proceeded expeditiously and cleverly than any other team in the against. If, in view of the warning served upon him, Union. This, oa Saturday, was fully demonstrated to the father at once supplied the needs of his children,the the Rovers undoing." I fancy Warrington pewle ' Guardians need not take any action. If the father failed will learn for the first time of this remarkable clever- to do that, and the relief was giveu,the Guardians were ness by the forwards. lip to SAturday they have often bound to make an effort to recover whatever expense failed badly in getting possession smartly, This has had been incurred. If they felt very strong l y in any particular case that it was undesirable to been /one of their few faults—heeling out. take legal proceedings, they must communicate -- directly with the Local Government Board and , We can