ildren wbuld take action in cases where ' Bradshaw, of Warrington, as hon. secretary the league children went without food through neglect. has been fortunate. A more enthusiastic sportsman it Mr. HICKMAN agreed with the suggestion that the would be difficult to find. and Mr. Bradshaw may be matter should be left in abeyance for the present. He did relied upon to infuse all his energy and ability into the , not see how they could form a committee until they i work. Mr. E. A. Connor has also been honoured by knew what it would have to do. ! election to the chairmanship. ' Mr. ASHCROFT thought the committee should be ' formed at once. He thought the Board would find that most of the children who would come for food would I be the children of parents who were already receiving I relief. ,It was decided to defer the matter. and the clerk was iustructecl to obtain a copy of the Order for each member.</p>
        <p>Considering the weather and the turf the• game was a very fair one particularly for a cup•final. Though close, the first half was always interesting by reason of the keen scrummaging, clever kicking, and resolute tickling. The second half was more open, and the Warrington backs showed what they could do in the way of combination. An excellent spirit was shown throughout the contest, and Referee Bruckshaw had an easy task which he fulfilled satisfactorily.</p>
        <p>Challenge cups are to be offered for competition in each division of the League, and they will become the absolute property of any teams winning them three years in succession. Gold medals will be presented to the winning team and silver medals to the runners-up. in each division. The cups will go to the clubs scoring the highest number of points during the competition,</p>
        <p>and the points awarded will be two for a win and one for a draw or tie. Abandoned thatches and losses will be ignored.</p>
        <p>The football cup has atlast come to Warrington. It would make the year 1905 still more memorable in sport ingcircles if the cricketers secured the West Lancashire trophy. May they attain their ambition! Percy Mason, the new professional, will assist the Warring- tonians this season and, as he made a very promising start at Bolton, great things ere expected of him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>Up to the present 21 clubs have signified their intention of taking part in the Works Association Com- petition, and as this number does not include all the works teams the draw for the first round of the com- petition has been adjourned until Monday. Four teams were drawn on Monday in order that to-day may not be a blank and the teams which will oppose each other on the King and Q'IPOII Hotel green are Longford Wire Works and Firth Mattress Co., and Pearson and Knowles (No. 1) and Naytors. The last named team stand little chance of getting over the first round, being matched against an excellent lot of players who succeeded last year in carrying off the premier honours. Still we are told that it is the unexpected which happens, and Naylor's may survive the meeting, though such a result, is highly improbable.</p>
        <p>The greatest inteVest will be centred in the other of the two matches as the teams could not have been more evenly matched if they had been specially selected to oppose eaoh other. As has been previously stated Long- ford were placed out of the running last year through an accident, and this season they mean to obtain satis- faction by making their presence felt. Whether they will succeed or not remains to he seen, at any rate, to- day they have no light task before them and, to use a sporting phrase, it appears to be anyone's game.</p>
        <p>• The season in connection with the War- rington and District League was successfully launched on Saturday, though two of the opening fixtures were postponed on account of the Northern Union football final. The attractions of the bowling green however proved to the Cemetery and Sankey Chapel House trundlers, stronger than the attraction of the football final, and the two teams met at the Cemetery Hotel,. The result of the meeting was not unexpected, the home team running out somewhat easy winners by 45 points. The players connected with both teams showed excellent form which promises well for a keen contest for leading positions. The details of the match are appended :</p>
        <p>CEM ETERY. CFIIP EL MANE. T. Berry V. Millar G. Ball J. Willett lO G. Rutter l5 W. Swarton l5 R. Beddard T. Ellison l5 J. Barton lO G. Holbrook l4 J. Hutchinson l5 E. Taylor l5 J. Goulden T. Bolton J. E. Harrison l5 J. Randlea lO W. Hill l3 W. Ellison E. Bothwell l2 J. Jackson l5</p>
        <p>Total 169</p>
        <p>Total _ __ 123</p>
        <p>Three matches will be decided to-day—Chapel House v. Penketh, Feamhead v. Bulls Head, Fearn- head v. Cemetery—and in each case the home team should win, though the Fearnhead team In both games will have to go the whole way if they are to win. Next Saturday, the first of the five mains in connection with the League will he held on the Ceme- tery green, and exciting playshould result.</p>
        <p>To-day the Liberal Club hold their second main of the season, and next week they will try conclusions with the Saracen's Head players.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>The unfavwtrable w3ather and the Cop final at Leeds resulted in the run arranged by the Warrington Cycling Club foy Saturdaybeing abandoned. Tuesday witnessed the first evening fixture of the season, and betwAen twenty and thirty members assembled at Sankey Chapel House where a highly successful "smoker" wag held. An excellent f:4ograrnme was contributed to by Messrs. W. H. Ireland, F. Pieldhoßse, J. L. D tvenport, G. H. Smith (vocalists) and A. 0. Clay (elocutionist). Mr. D. Hodge (captain) presided over the gathering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Vagrancy Problem.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Board of Guardians met at the Work- house on Wednesday, Mr. J. Biltinge presiding. There were also present:—Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Rev. Dr. Mercer, !ilessrs. W. Hickman (viol-chairman). A. J. Jolley, T. H. Ashuroft, J. ?Alton, S. Davies, R. Bolton, G. Thompson, G. Mather, W. Flannagan, A. Brooks, R. Forshaw, 'M. W. Seton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>AN INDriPOSED MEMBEn.</head>
        <p>AN INDriPOSED MEMBEn.</p>
        <p>Mr. THOMPSOV asked the Chairman whether Mr. Turton, who had been absent from the Board meetings for some time, was unwell. The CIiA.IRMAN : I am sorry to say he is rather indisposed, but he is improving, and hopes to attend to the business shortly. nA PADGATE SCHOOLS COMNIITTEE. At a meeting or Padgate Schools Committee it was unanimously resolved that Mr. George Mnther be elected chairmin, and Mrs. Clay was unanimously elected to the vice-chair.</p>
        <p>The minutes were confirmed. HOUSE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee elected Mr. R,. Forshaw chairman and Mr. G. Thompson vice-chairman.lt was decided that Mrs. Clay, Mr. Thompson and the master should attend a conference at Manchester to discuss the vagrant problem and the desirability of securing unformity of treatment, especially in Mr. Jenner Fast's district. The minutes were approved. CONTRACTS. a</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported—tl;;;;CeW of a letter from the Local Government Board asking for a return relating to contracts for the execution of works, with reference particularly to the working conditions and wages paid. He had replied stating that the Board had no such con- tracts pending THE VAGRANCY PROBLEM. The CLERK read a letter stating that at a conference held at Manchester under the auspices of the number 11 district of the Association of Workhouse Masters and Matrons, to consider the vagrancy problem, the follow- ing resolution was passed :—That the members of this Association respectfully suggest to the Guardians present that they should press home to their respective Boards the importance of securing this district a uniform treatment of the vagrant, especially in matters relating to detention diet, and tasks, and further to ask them to exert their best endeavours to arouse the atten- tion of the responsible authorities to the urgent necessity which exists for National reforms in this</p>
        <p>direction. As a representative of the Board at the conference, Mr. THOMPSON said the question of vagrancy had been on his mind for a very long time. Tramps could be divided Into two classes—the professional and the bona fide. He thought something should be done very quickly to put an end to professional tramps. They were a nuisance to people on the highway, and no doubt they were doing a great amount of injury all over the country. He thought it would be advisable to bring the queetion before the House Committee when he could explain more fully the nature of the proceed- ings at the conference and formulate 1, resolntion to introduce at a later Board meeting. No G :le from Mr. Jenner Fast's district had, he believed, been asked to give evidence before the Commission on the question. .The Master of the Warrington Workhouse was the secretary of the Association under whose auspices Saturday's conference was held, and he would propose that the Clerk should write to Mr. Jenner Fust suggesting that Mr. Awbery should be called to give evidence before the Commission. Those who heard Mr. Awbery speak on Saturday would be fully convinced that he was very well up on the subject and would give evidence which would be of very great service to the committee. Mrs. CLAY secondea the proposition, which was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>Milliady Pokiada, a Greek. was sentenced to six weeks' imprisonment, in London on Thursday, for stabbing a prison warder who was sewing good conduct stripes on his arm at Wormwood Scrnbbs Prison. The convict, it appeared, thought the stripes meant addi- tional imprisonment.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S GOLDEN BUTTER</p>
        <p>BISCUITS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
