      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE. Love's Hipocrisy.</head>
        <p>PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE. Love's Hipocrisy.</p>
        <p>![By Godfrey Dean.]</p>
        <p>"What do the papers say?" Phyllis asked. Her in i interest in them s parely second hand—that is, through me." " "Similar things in similar ways," I said.</p>
        <p>"But chiefly 'Merely that the situation is grave. But the gravity of that statement has been its iteration and reiteration." "The situation?" "The papers say the situation; but they are always open to contradiction." • "Isn't it true, then?" "Perfectly. But they mistake the situation. Parliament is sitting." "I did not know that was a serious matter." "It's because it, isn't that it is."</p>
        <p>In-..-ans;;;;;7;1;474-rte;Ion-i.added, "We can do without Parliament very well. , Hasn't it been demon- strated times out of number? Periodically Parliament dissolves for the recess. Yet ,everything goes on as usual. People buy and sell, dihe and go to the theatre, and—make love.</p>
        <p>" Really !"--in a detached manner. " Of course one can always see that,"</p>
        <p>"It is a mistake," she said. " I don't think so. To see lovers constantly—to intercept affectionate glances between two people in- different of any other world but that of their own—is to realise the unimportance of the important." " I meant," Phyllis said, " that you—one, lather (she corrected)—ought not to notice it." "One may see without seeing, and the art (by the way) is worth cultivating. Though, when lovers are Pbout, it is to the credit of the ordinary man that he is blind to the obvious. "Love inspires hypocrisy. I can be unconscious of what is passing, particularly when with you." She pretends not to notice the last remark. " Hypocrisy ? That may or may not be a proper confession.' "I'm quite sure there is nothing wrong in /being just a little hypocritical about same things ; it hurts no one, and may be achieved simply by ignoring the fact." . .</p>
        <p>"I can't tllink," reflectively, "that hypocrisy is ' right." Well, you shall decide for yourself; though, usually, conscience is the better judge. In this case, however, I have no conscience." A. reproving glaace from Phyllis but I remained .callous —to the, reproof. "You shall have my dis- interested opinion," she said. "It doesn't promise well." "What do you expect of a judge? "—which was unanswerable. _ _</p>
        <p>"This is a case in point," I said. "Briefly, when l am with you, I pretend that I am interested in external affairs—things, for instance, which you might or , might not be interested in." "I consider," she said, "that the hypocrisy is wrong." " I accept your decision," I said, "but (firmly) I retain my own opinion. We can agree to differ.' "Certainly," she said. "Of course, as I have said before, arum's point of view is in some respects necessarily different from a woman's. "Yes." she said, unmoved by this appeal. "And I agreed—thou." "The more I think of it," I said, "the less import- ant it seems whether lam hypocritical or no: It is harmless." "To me," she said, "it grows in importance. I dislike the word." "it is such a trifle; moreover, I am justified by the fact that creates it."</p>
        <p>"If you think so, then I Can't help it." "It is too early for crocuses in the park, isn't it? " she asked almost immediately after. The suddenness of this remark, and the acceptance of the position which it suggested was too much for me.</p>
        <p>" Phyllis," I said, "I am absolutely indifferent at the present moment whether crocuses are growing or not. She was surprised at my vehemence, but not dis- pleased. " You are surprised," I said, "that I should say such a thing; but I was compelled." • "I forgive you," she said ; but—you need not be hypocritical.' Her manner was very provoking ; while, recalling the association in which they were first used, it seemed possible to put another construction on her words, " I'll not be hypocritical, then," I said. " You won't pretend to be interested in external affairs," she repeated. Light at last to my understandin; ! Phyllis's natural delicacy often leads me astray. " No," I said. " I'll not make the smallest pretence of being interested when it is not necessary. • I can see no reason for it now. Hypocrisy is wrong." " Are you quite convinced ?' she asked, smiling at my confidence. Quite." After all, it was only my disinterested opinion, you know," she said. "You appealed to me as a judge." ' "If that was your opinion as a judge," I said, " then I shall appeal to a higher court." a What is that ?" 1 "The Court of Love. I shall appeal to you per- sonally. From you, at least, I ought to get justice." She waited:expectantly. " I wish to know," I said, "whether, when in the 'company of the lady one loves, one ought to pretend to be interested in external affairs ?" Phyllis said," I should think only when it was necessary." So if she infers that hypocrisy is wrong, then it is— good society notwithstanding. " That, however," she added, tentatively, "is an interested opinion." I did not object to it. (These articles originally appeared in the "London Echo.") (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION CHAMPIONSHIP. --- Warrington " A" Team's Victory.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION CHAMPIONSHIP. --- Warrington " A" Team's Victory.</p>
        <p>By defeating Chadderton on Saturday, Warrington " A " team beca:ne the chathpions of the LanciLsbire Combination —an honour which by excellent work throughout the season they have Nveli merited. Had the issue of the match been reversed NVigan "A" would have secured the premier position, and in view of the irupor- tance of the engagement it was not surprising that some 2,000 spectators should assemble on the Wilierspool enclosure. The teams lined out as follows: Chadderton—Sidebottom, full back ; Lewis, Long- bottom, Saunders, and Brown, threequarters ; Carter and Cash, half-backs; Stubbs, Moorhouse, Roscoe, Todd and Douglas, forwards. Warrington—linight,full back ; Harris, Berry,Hewitt, and Jordan, threequarters ; J. Davies and Duckworth, half-backs ; Forster, Cook, Ashton, Fairhurst and Lunt, forwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington started off in a surprising manner, Sidebottom being tackled on the Chaddertou line while attempting to reply to a kick by Harris. For some minutes Warrington kept their advantageous position until a free kick brought the visitors a little relief. Berry a few minutes later had a shot at goal the ball however, going wide. The restart witnessed a sensa- tional piece of work, the Warrington forwards dribbling the ball ovet the visitors line for Ashtorb to score near the corner. Berry added the goal from a difficult angle. Cnadderton teak up the attack but were no match for Warrington who easily kept them out. There was no further score before half time and the teams changed ends with Warrington leading by 5 points to nil. Re-starting, Warrington at once assumed the aggres- sive, and having kept up the pressure for some time, Forsterscored as the result of some good forward work. Berry hit the post with the kick at goal. A few minutes later Berry put in a splendid run, and ended by finding touch near the visitors' line. From the scrim- mage Jordan made a strung attempt to score, only being pulled up when on the line. .Lunt was injured, and for some time Warrington were without his services. Despite this,however, they were more than a match for Chadde r ton and had there been more combination amongst the backs a big score must have resulted. As it was Jordan was the only one to increase the lead of the home team, scoring near the corner as the result of some loose play. Knight was unlucky with the kick at goal the ball re- bounding off the crossbar. Shortly afterwards the whistle was blown for time, and Warrington ran out winners of the combination shield and medals, by one goal three tries (11 points) to nil.</p>
        <p>EL ANTIC STOCEING9, KNEE OAPS, ttc.—For Varlcoa Vela and all eases of Weakness and' lwelllng of the Le 4 Sprains, i&amp;c They are porous. light In texture. and in expensive, and are -dr‘vra on like an ordinary sto3 king Prices 4s. 6d., 7s, 65. and 10s. 6d. vie]. Postage free.— Wain &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument and Truss Makers, 98, Renshaw at., Livezpoo'.—S.dvt. (11</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Sunday School Anniversary.</p>
        <p>Rev, W. F. Adeney and the Need for</p>
        <p>Individualism In Religion.</p>
        <p>The'anniversary of the Wycliffe Sunday School was celebrated on Sunday by the holding of special services in the church. The past year has been a most successful one in the history of the school and great interest was taken in the services on Sunday. The preacher was the Rev. W. F. Adeney, principal of Lancashire Independent College, who in the morning addressed a large congregation from the words" For, behold,l create new heavens and new earth," Isaiah 5Gc. 17v. His address in the evening was based on Hebrews 11c., 13v. "They were strangers and pilgrims on the earth." He would, he said, take Bunyan's " Pilgrim's Progress "as an illustration of the difference between modern religion and Puritanism. He would discuss the question of religion and not of theology or creed, and of Bunyan's religion and not his Calvinism. One great objection to the" Pilgrims' Progress "was its so called individualism. There were those who said the great business of religion was the Church. The Church had its place but it was not the business of religion. Could we altogether afford to give up that individualism professed by our Puritan forefathers? There were people to-day who taught that there was no salvation out of the Church while others went even further and taught that the way to salvation was within the church. In our churches thero was a great danger of our losing our individuality. He had been astonished in the Esta'Aished Church to hear whole congregations join in a general confession and say they had strayed like lost sheep,and call. themselves miserable sinners. Nearer home he had been equally astonished to hear congregations join in the consecration hymn "Take my life and let it be consecrated, Lord, to Thee." How different it would be if the congregations were to break up into units and each individually to appeal to their God. The difference between the modern religion was the difference between the religion of the multitude and the religion of the soul. He believed that if we never worshipped God except in congregations we never worshipped , Him at all. Jesus Christ never commanded public I worship. He approved of it, but at the same time He explained the necessity for private worship. We must become Christians by Individual decision and a personal Christian life, and when we did that, and not before, it would follow that we would really join in Christian fellowship and strengthen Church association. Another objection to the " Pilgrim's Progress " was its selfish- ness. A man learnt that the town in which he lived was to be destroyed, and failing to persuade his wife and family to accompany him he fled alone... That was, apparently, a strong objection to the moral tone of the book, but it was necessary to understand the allegory. The city of destruction meant the leading of an idle dissolute life and it was from this that Christian fled. There was in that a personal sense of religionjwhich we had not amongst us at the present day. We talked of Christian work but it was not until we owned a personal spiritual feeling that we might be fit to do some service for others. If we were to live the highest and best life we must to a certain extent be pilgrims and strangers, and as such must not always eKpect to be at our ease. Throughout this life we would constantly be faced with troubles but the eternal life would be all the sweeter for the difficulties of this life. Any life which did not follow Christ was full of disappointment but if we joined in the pilgrimage of Jesus Christ eternal life would in the end be ours. At both services thelmusic was of a special character. In the morning the choir ably sang the anthem" Sing 0 heavens " (Sullivan) the solo being taken by Mr. A. Griffiths. In the evening the anthem "As the heart pants" (Meni^lsitlion) was sung, Mr. Griffiths again being the soloist. A special service was held in the afternoon at which there was a large attendance. Mr. W. Roberts presided land an interesting address was given by the Rev. J. F. Matthews. The solo The good Shepherd " (Barn) was effectively sung by Mr. A. Cotterill, while Mrs. R. H. Pearson gave great pleasure with her rendering of the song "Jerusalem" (Parker). Mr. W. H. Payton mus. bac., presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Rev. H: Dunnico and the Social Gospel.</head>
        <p>Rev. H: Dunnico and the Social Gospel.</p>
        <p>Reference to the Cup Final.</p>
        <p>The last of the present series of Parr Hall services* was held on Sanday,the hall being well filled. The Rev* H. Dunnico conducted, and in his address stated that financial considerations, coupled with failing health on his part,had led them to discontinue the services for the present. He hoped, however, to recommence them next , September. He was more than ever convinced that preaching to-day was not at a discount, and that the Gospel of Jesus Christ had not lost its Ipower to attract the great masses of mankind. Personally there were several things he had endeavoured to reach. First and foremost he had preached what was termed a social gospel. In his opinion the Gospel of Christ had a message for all men in every age and con- dition of life. If Jesus Christ were here to-day he had no doubt as to what He would say about such things. He would say that men ought to have regular work and that no honest people should ever be hungry : that masters should pay a living wage, and that men should in return give an honest days work. He would say that no man should invest money in that which wrecked homes and ruin( d man- hood ; that the drinking customs of this country ought to be abolished, and every sweating den destroyed ; that persons who bought sweated goods because they were cheap were just as blameworthy as the men who manufactured them or sold them for the sake of profits. That was the social gospel hehad proclaimed and it was part and parcel of the Gospel of Jesus arist. Then there was the mad craze for pleasure. Thousands of young men throughout the country could think of nothing else but football. Football in itself was innocent enough, but it, was being pursued to such an extent as to become pernicious and harmful. In- cidentally he might mention that some £3,000 or £.1.000 had been spent the previous day by Warrington football enthusiasts. in view of the fact that people were starving and children were ill-clad, was not this sheer waste ? if they had taken a walk over Hill Cliffe they would have received more good than they got by standing in a dense crowd in a downpour of rain for two hours. Would any sensible man dare to say that War- rington was from a mental and moral standpoint £4OOO better off than it was on Friday? In conclusion be urged that after all the soul of all improvement was the improvement of the soul. Outward forms and con- ditions were useful—even necessary—but the main factor was spiritual regeneration. He appealed to all to live nobler lives, and in wishing his congregation good-bye for a little while, hoped they would not waste their Sunday evenings upon the streets, but attend some place of worship—be it Nonconformist, Anglican, or Catholic, —for only by doing so would they be kept in constant remembrance of the fact that " man doth not live by bread alone."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ST, AUSTIN'S WARD VACANCY.</head>
        <p>ST, AUSTIN'S WARD VACANCY.</p>
        <p>The election in Si;. Austin's Ward takes place next Monday, and a considerable amount of interest appears to be manifested in the contest. The candidates are Dr. Joseph (C) and Ur. E. Horrocks (L), and the election address of each gentleman appears in our advertising columns.. Dr. Joseph states that he is a determined advocate of economy Aga the spending of public monies; he is of orPffon that every step possible should be taken to further reduce the high rates, and he promises to give careful consideration to efficiency and progress in the management of the affairs of the Borough. " With regard to the vital necessity for absolute probity in public work " he states " I am opposed to any member of the Council benefiting in any way from his position." Mr. florrocks informs the electors that he believes in absolute purity of government, and further states that he strongly favours a further extension of municipal enterprise, trade union rate of wages, and an eight-hour day for all Corporation workmen, direct employment of *labour by the Corporation, the feeding of under-fed school children, abolition of all insanitary property under the Working Classes' Dwellings Acts, free education, immediate construction of a healthy and commodious lodging house on modern principles, free baths, eheaper gas, branch libraries, useful work for the unemployed, taxing of land values, a stringent Fair Wage Clause in all Corporation contracts, reduc- tion in train fares.</p>
        <p>During the week Mr. Horrocks li.as held several largely attended meetings in the ward and has explained h's views on various municipal questions. The palling stations and officials will ba as follows: Cairo-street school, presiding officer, Mr. A. Starkey, poll clerk, Mr. Samuel Wright; Thewlis-street school, presiding officers, Messrs. H. Lawrence and W. H. J. Jenkins, poll clerks, Messrs. Douglas Skelton and. Walter Sumner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION.—On Wednesday evening there was 4 social gathering in the Hill Cliffe schoolroom of the Christian Endeavour Society and Sunday School teachers, in order to celebrate the recent marriage of three persons connected with the chapel. Refreshments were served by Mrs. Hughes, the Misses Mellor and Mrs. Wheeler, while the company were entertained by Mr. A. Arnold, and Mr. Wheeler. Towards the close of the proceedings Mrs. J. S. Hughes presented Mr. and Mrs. George Moss, Mr. and Mr. W. A. Wilde respec- tively with, a teachers Bible and a sitting-room clock. Congratulatory speeches were made and a very enjoy- ' able evening was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WORD TO THE WISE.</head>
        <p>A WORD TO THE WISE.</p>
        <p>The fickle nature of the climate of this country often gives rise to a variety of ailments and complaints, which assuming at first the form of only a slight indisposition, if neglected and unheeded may become the germ of I serious diseases, which will undermine the constitution, and finally result in a long and lingering illness, and perhaps terminate fatally. The variable temperature and the changeable weather of the last month or so, makes it incumbent on all of us to exercise wise and prudent precautions to counteract the evil effects which the weather may have had upon our health and comfort. Already we frequently hear such complaints as "No appetite," "These frequent headsches,' "This languid feeling," and dozens of other expressions which all point to the urgent need of a good Tonic. Now there are several tonic mixtures offered to the Public, but none which have been so uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. This Preparation is acknowledged to be The Best Remedy of The Age for Nervousness Weakness. Chest Affections, Palpitation of the Heart, Indigestion, Liver Complaint, and Influensa. The numerous cases of marvellous cures effected, and the restoration to perfect health, of those who had long suffered and had vainly tried other remedies, can be accounted for by simnly stating the effect which Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has upon the System. Ist. It strikes at the source of the disease, and by removing the causes of disease, the evil effects soon vanish. 2nd. It strengthens that part of the system which is weakest, and therefore, most liable to the attacks of colds and all diseases. 3rd. It purifies the blood, and thus gives new life andsforce to all parts of the body, for disease cannot exist where there is a free circulation of pure blood 4th. It gives healthy action to the digestive organs and to the liver, thus aiding to keep away Indigestion and the host of different forms of disease which re- nit from it, sth. By removing impurities, strengthening the weak parts of the system, and purifying the blood,the human frame is well fortified to withstand the attacks of disease. It is repelled, and its place taken by health and strength, through the effective action of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable "ronic. Sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each, or will be sent carriage free for these prices, direct from the Sole Proprietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Com- pany, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. Beware of Imitations. See that you get the genuine Preparation with the name, " Gwilym Evans" on Label, Stamp, and Bottle, without which none is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>CAUGHT IN THF. ACT. At the Warrington police Court on Thursday Elizabeth W. Morgan, Ardwick, Manchester, was charged with stealing a purse containing 95. Old. and a gold brooch from the person of Mary Jane Dean. She pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that for some considerable time the market had on Wednesdays been pestered by pick-pockets, and the police had been baffled in their attempts to discover the culprits. The day previously Mrs. Dean was in the market and was making a purchase at a stall when she felt a hand in her pocket She saw prisoner take her hand out of her pocket. Prisoner was wearing a long cape in which were two large pockets. Mrs. Dean asked for the return of her purse when the prisoner screamed and dropped the purse on the ground. Detective Clough, who was hearing a •complaint from another woman who had lost her purse, heard the commotion and went and arrested the prisoner Mr. Browne who appeared on behalf of the prisoner said that the woman had taken to drink, and on Wednesday was under the influence of drink and did not know what she was doing. She had no need to steal as her husband gave her plenty of money.—A fine of £5 and costs was inflicted.</p>
        <p>NEGLECT OF CI-MORE:I. At the Warrington Police Court on Thursday, Frederick Norton, Elizabeth-street, was charged with neglecting and illtreating his children.—He pleaed guilty.—Mr. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C.t and stated that in February last the prisoner was served with a summons for neglecting his children. He however refused to answer to the summons and was arrested at Rochdale on Wednesday. Hewlsad previ- ously been imprisoned for neglecting his children, but it:did not seem to have done him any good. On several occasions he had gone home drunk, turned his children out of the house and kept thorn out for the best part of the night. On February 11th he turned them out and Inspector Cox on going to speak to him found him to be mad drunk. Prisoner worked at Monks, Hall and Company's works and every dinner time the men were met by a man who lent them money charging 3d. for every ls. advanced. The result was that on Satur..lay the prisoner instead of having 28s. had only 15s. in his pocket.—Prisoner pleaded bard to be given another chance, and upon his promising to turn over a new leaf he was bound over for 3 months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>Monoxv.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Fmncomb and Smethurs t,Councillor Wilkinson,Messrs. H. Roberts, Hayward, Thornton, Pilling, Geddes Reynolds, Wright and W. Roberts, and Dr. Fox. THE BEGGING NUISANCE.—James Leonardy, a well known character, was charged with begging in Sankey- street, on Saturday night.—The Chief Constable remarked that whenever there was anything .special on in Warrington a number of local loafers like the prisoner stopped occasional visitors to the town to beg from them. A week or two pre- viously there had been a fatal accident, one "cadger " following and so exasperating a man that he pushed him down, the" cadger" sustaining injuries from which he died.—P.C. Clough proved the ase.—Prisoner who had been 35 times previously convicted was sent to gaol for 14 days with hard labour. THEFT FROM A PAWNSHOP.—Henry Caffnev was charged with stealing a man's coat and vest,- value 6s. Ild, from outsidethe pawnshop of Mrs. Houghton, Mersey street. The chief constable stated that on the 13thl March Mrs. Houghton's assistant saw the articles mentioned in the charge hanging safe outside the shop door and subsequently missed them. It was later found that they had been pawned at Messrs. Morton's shop in Knutsford-road by a man named Maguire who when questioned said he had pawned them on behalf of the prisoner. Prisoner who had been convicted 15 times of theft was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LOST BALLOON.</head>
        <p>A LOST BALLOON.</p>
        <p>On Easter Monday the large captive balloon sus- pended above the Aston -Villa football ground, at Birmingham, broke loose from its moorings, and has not been seen or heard of since. The balloon is about eight feet high, and bears an inscription, "Royal Enfield Bicycles, Made Like a Gun." Apparently the holiday spirit had overmastered the balloon, or perchance the earthquakes in the Midlands were responsible, but, at any rate, if any readers should come acros the wanderer, they will oblige by communicoting with the Enfield Cycle Co., Ltd., Redditch, as to its whereabouts.</p>
