      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>the investigation if thought advisable, was agreed to unanimously and without discussion.</p>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL BOWLING GREEN.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBRIITON asked the Mayor, as Chairman of the Town Hall, Parks and Batas Commitiee,when the municipal bowling green in Victoria Park was to be opened._</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : It is not quite ready yet. As soon as it is lit to play upon it will be opened. Councillor PEmusaToN : I have some 'information regarding charges. The MAYOR : The committee will be pleased to re- ceive the information. Bring it before the next General Purposes Committee.</p>
        <p>HUNGRY SCEIOOL CLILDEN. Councillor WRIGHT had given notice to move "That in the opinion of the Warrington Town Council the Education (Provision of IVleals) 13111, 1905 should be considered by the House of commons without delay, in view of the serious nature of the problem with which it deals, and this Council resolves to petition the Prime Minister to grant every facility for this Bill being passed this session." He said it had come to his know- ledge that afternoon that the Local Government Board had made an order with reference to this matter, and as he had not had time to read it yet he wished, without prejudice, to withdraw the motion. This was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>TRAGIC AFFAIR AT RUNCORN. I Young Child Shockingly Injured. ,</head>
        <p>TRAGIC AFFAIR AT RUNCORN. I Young Child Shockingly Injured. ,</p>
        <p>BDY OF TEN ARRESTED.</p>
        <p>Tragedies are so uncommon in Runcorn that the occurrence of one on Monday afternoon set the town in a state of unusual excitement. The victim is a little girl of three years 3 named Ethel Ciatley, daughter of ' Thomas Gatlev, of Mill-street, ana granddaughter of a well-known confectioner in Church-street. Ethel is described as a beautiful child, with light curly hair and a sunny disposition. She was missed from her home about noon, and afterwards a hue and cry was set up to kindlier. Her home is on the banks of the Ship Canal, but as these are well protected there was not much fear that she had wandered into the waterway. About half-past four a boy, Whose name has not been ascertained, found the little girl in an extraordinary position. She was lying face downwards between two mounds of chemical waste near the Runcorn Alkali Works of the United Alkali Co. She was at the bottom of a mound about 30 feet high, on which the children of the neighbourhood are wont to play in considerable numbers. The poor little creature was almost undressed. Her shoes and stockings had been removed as well as her dress. On her forehead, ovet the right eye' was a fearful gash, from which the blood had evidently flowed fteely, and, needless to say, she was quite unconscious. Help was quickly at hand, and she was speedily carried to the surgery of Dr. Robinson, and afterwards taken home.</p>
        <p>It appears that a boy name-I Fred Waker, aged ten years. of weak intellect, son of Thomas Walker, of Princess-street, was seen with the girl in the Belvedere, which is near her home. Afterwards they were seen together in Greenway-road, which leads to the chemical waste mound. What other clues the police possess is unknown, but they have evidently collected sufficient evidence to lead them to arrest the boy Walker. BOY BEFORE THE MAGIBTRATES.</p>
        <p>CONDITION OF THE INJURED CHILD.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court on Tuesdly a consider- able time elapsed before any magistrate put in an appearance. At 12.15, before Mr. E. unison (in the chair) and Mr. T. D. Timmins, the boy Walker was i brought into court. He is rather swarthy n appearance with a prominent forehead and sunken eyes. His fea- tures betoken one of weak intellect. Stepping on to a stand near the magistrates' table, he glanced round the court with an unassuming air. . . .</p>
        <p>In answer to the magiarates' clerk, he gave his name as Fred. Walker, and said that on the 4th inst. he would be eleven years of age. He was then charged with feloniously attempting to kill and murder a little girl named Ethel Gatley, aged three years. Superintendent Bunion said that the prisoner *as the son of Thomas Walker, 22, Princess-street. He was a boy who would not attend school, and, furthermore, was an annoyance to the children playing in the street. From information in the possession of the police it was gleaned that the little girl was seen in the company of the boy Walker playing in the neighbourhood of Belve- dere, of Greenway-road. They were afterwards seen going to a mound of soap waste close by. Nothing seemed to have been heard or seen of them for some hours afterwards. The girl's mother became anxious about her child's long absence, and after going to her mother-in-law's, to see if she was there,and not finding her, she gave information to the police. In the meantime a young girl named Elizabeth Hayes had found the child on the soap waste, covered over with a dirty piece of oilcloth. The cloth was taken away, and a horrible sight met her gaze. Ethel, who by this time must have been lying on the ground for about three 'hours, was in a horribly battered condition about the face and head, caused, presumably with a brick. Over the left eye was a huge gash, and one behind the head. ' Her nose, mouth, and eyes were much discoloured. All her clothes were stripped from her and placed in a heap close by. A huge brick was also spattered with blood, and was produced in court. With all possible speed she was taken to Dr. Robinson in an unconscious condition, and after a period of artificial respiration the girl was brought round Three pieces of brick were found, and those also were covered with blood The boy was arrested later on. and examined by Drs. M'Dugall and Blunten, who certified hiin to be sane and neither an idiot nor imbecile.</p>
        <p>George Laxton, about thirteen years of age, of 83 Greenway-road, said that he saw the boy and girt in comnaay together between 12.15 and 1.45 on some waste land near Oakhill-road. They 'passed him about ten yards distant, and be shouted,'" Where are you going to, Walker ?" to which no answer was made. _ _</p>
        <p>..... Mrs. Francis Gatley. wife of George Gatley, stated that she lived in Mill-street, and the little girl mentioned in the charges was her daughter, who, at a quarter to twelve, was playing in the street. She saw her daughter brought home by her husband about five o'clock. Witness described the wounds. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Jackson (magistrates' clerk) to witness: How is she this morning? Mrs Gatley: She ia no better. • - - -</p>
        <p>Prisoner was remanded for seven days.</p>
      </div>
