        <head>DAINTY sikNll3larthl 'FrUE.E.</head>
        <p>DAINTY sikNll3larthl 'FrUE.E.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Future of the Pantasaph Girls.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present—Messrs. A. R. Norman (in the chair), T. Baxter, T. Wright, J. Stokes, C. E. Linaker, sen., J. Douglas, W. M. Morris, J. W. Temperley, S. Taylor, J. Hallows, C. Rovlance, J. Spruce, J. Whitley, J. Collins, G. Pining, G. Egerton-Warburton,J. L. Jones, J.Smith, J. Bolshaw, J. B. Chadwick, T. Antwis, T. Guest, H. Mawdsley, J. H. Davies, H. M. Davies, H.Eyes, P.Moun- field. A. Broome, W. Harrisqn, E. Bretherton, R. A. Chrimes, J. Fryer, J. Rutter, R. Billingtod, G. F.Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), and J. Cunnah (workhouse master.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FRODSGAM RELIEVING OFFICER.</head>
        <p>FRODSGAM RELIEVING OFFICER.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote confirming the appointment of Mr. Pearson as relieving officer for Frodsham.</p>
        <p>THE 'GIRLS IN PANTASAPH ORPHANAGE. Mr. Spruce had given notice that he would move "That the Chairman. Vice-Chairman, and the Clerk be I appointed a special deputation to visit Pantasaph Orphanage to ascertain whether the elder girls at present chargeable to this Union can be placed in situations to enable them to earn their own livings." In view of the notice the Clerk wrote to the Superioress of the Orphanage, and had receiveda report , from her with regard to the children under her charge from the Runcorn Union. One of the girls was engaged fo a situation. Two were in a delicate state of health. 0 n was not fit to be sent to a situation while two would b ready for situation while two would be ready for situa tionS this year. The other girls were still at school. Mr. Spruce said the report from the orphanage somet what modified his resolution. He therefore moved "That the Board learns with gratitude that One of the girls in the orphanage is to be put out to service. The Board expresses its satisfaction with the authorities of the orphanage and urges them to get those girls over 15 years of age, who were able to work, some employ- ment. He thought that when children reached the age of 15 years they should be °amino.' their own livings. The education sub-committee had many applications monthly from parents who desired to take advantage of a chow, in the County Council bye-laws, to allow their children to start work when they had reached the age of 113 years. He thought therefore they were justified in getting the girls in the orphanage out to work before they reached the age of 16. All the girls who had gone' from the institution had turned out well. Mr. Smith seconded the motion. In answer to the chairman Mr. Spruce said that in his motion he only referred to girls who were in good health. Mr. Norman said he was quite in accord with the resolution. He thought that any girls who were in good health should be doing something when they reached the age of 15 years. Mr. Morris said he thought the resolution wac hardly needed as the Clerk had written recently to the superioress of the- institution very much on the same lines. In answer to a member Mr. Wright said there was no difficulty at all in finding situations for the girls. Mr. Hallows said he felt vary strongly in that matter and proposed that the age limit should be fixed at 14 years. A workingman's child had to be at work when it reached the age of 14 years and he did not see why the girls in the Pantasaph Orphanage should be treated differently. The Chairman : I also feel strongly and therefore support the resolution finding the age limit at 15. In aninstitution like that we ought not to fix the standard or the lives suggested by some members of the Board. Mr. Spruce: Workingmen's children, when they go to work have a horse to go to. Girls from the orpanage were altogether in a different position. Mr. Norman said in dealing with the children under the care of the Board they were dealing with men and women of the future and if they were to err it should be on the generous rather than on the parsimonious side. On a vote being taken Mr. Spruce's motion was carried by a large majority. BUCKLEY HALL ORPHANAGE. The clerk intimated that the Brothers of Charity (Buckley Hall Orphanage) had written asking that the contribution from the Board should be increased to 7s. per head per week.</p>
        <p>Mr. Norman said from enquiries which'had be it had been found that 53. per week was the 0 9.. for children in such institutions. Mr. Baxter moved that the payment to the °l.!' be not increased. Mr. Browne seconded and the propOsLtt carried. UNDERFED CHILDREN. • The Clerk read an Order fr the L al vo to' .om e oc Board with regard to underfed children elementary schools. Under the Order relie'iortA given when application was made by an educatd owielre to children. Where children were underfe reco,,ool the neglect of the parents the cost could b 3, the tilind from the parents in full. The Board hOpeuoicat dians would co-operate with the Local 050 Authority in dealing with really deserving "c underfed children. " The Chairman remarked that the Board IV° e Id to carry out the Order. der It was deciled to send copies of the , relieving officers.</p>
        <p>Concert at St. Peter's. . A concert was given in St. Peter's sell° s Monday, in aid of the Young Women's Clasosa'4.01 , room was crowded, and the entertainment 0 very enjoyable character. A quartet entitieuoo, the budding bloom. of May," b Mrs C. O. St s jG. Adams, Mr. Fieldlniiy se, and fr. 31°°,r„ec,Ile excellently rendered. Miss Stanley gave ArVildelli played mandoline solo, while a duet lartg by Mr. Fieldhouse and Mrs. W. B. Robbei loudly applauded. " Zuyder-Zee," sung bei, eve' Stone, pleased the audience, while later in 31r* she rendered with effect " Oh, Mister 3100,13' Mores sang excellently " Tike Seaside Du vo " Cupid at the ferry," by Miss Brundrett, '4ll rendered. Mr. Victor Roberts was sucne' his humorous sketches. A character Sketell 1:9" in excellent style by Mrs. C. 6. Stone. t,11...)). Adams and Francomb, Mr. Fieldhouse, Mr. Sutton, and Critchley. " Farewell, San," was well sung by Miss G. Adams. - (in character) brought the Concert to a cl°se.</p>
