      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Opening Run.</head>
        <p>The Opening Run.</p>
        <p>, What memories does the first run with the dear eld club conjure up in the mind! First there was feeling of gladness at becoming a clubman, Ind enjoying the boon companionship of others hose hobby and sympathies lay in the same !iirection. Then the pleasure of setting out from -headquarters, with the captain leading his little band, and the sub-captain acting as "whipper-in," and seeing that the pace was not too fast for the •Yonn6er members; the pride of making a good. displa6y as the city or town streets were slowly tngotiated, until the fresh and open country per- /bitted of a brisker ace;p the merry chat on the</p>
        <p>Wan and pleasant interchange of cycling topics; he subtle jokes of the club's own funny man, who Invariably remarks that he is "baked" when -attacking a hill, but is usually next to the captain at the finish. Then comes the halt at some quaint old inn in a pretty village, where a right good Xiiglish welcome is offered, and where always the good digestion of the invigorated rider waits upon an appetite of Gargantuan proportions. Of the ride home, the thoughts of having made many new and congenial friends, and the peaceful slumber that follows the enjoyable spin into the country, o.nly those who have tasted of the delights of club life know their full value.</p>
