      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>,11. Lamp Tip.</head>
        <p>,11. Lamp Tip.</p>
        <p>Nothing„ perhaps, is more irritating to the cyclist than a-lamp which refuses to burn brightly after The match has been applied to it, Assuming, of course, that there is a good supply of oil in the little receptacle, that the wick is neither too long tor too short and is well trimmed, and that the larnP itself is free from dust or dirt, there is no reason why the illuminant should not fulfil its AntY. Some years ago I was very much puzzled about my lamp's lack of energy, as it were, and Inore by mere chance than anything else I dis- tovered the remedy. It was on a very broad and PPon common on a lovely summer's night that the discovery was made. I applied the match as usual, ;but .could not get the wick to light up beyond a glimmer • So exasperated was I that I gave up the task as hopeless and proceeded homeward, chancing tO luck and the bright moon to find my way. I d not ridden far when I noticed that the road in font \was brightly marked out, and to my utter autonishment found the lamp burning splendidly. ile sum total of the whole business was that the L'arup required a draught to encourage it, which it Lt a d duly received in the few yards ‘I had riddea. rM y tip to riders, therefore, is: Do not give the task 1115 as hopeless, but try the "draught" dodge. Iv 449 Amp put of ten this will put matters right.</p>
      </div>
