      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>, FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. W. J. WIGFIELD.—The remains of the late Mr. W. J. Wigfield. late secretary to the Penketh Tanning Company, Limited, were interred in the Wallasey Cemetery on Saturday afternoon. The Rev. John Gunnell (resident Wesleyan minister at Penketh)and the Rev. J. E.Dison officiating.Mr.Gunnell delivered a short address in the cemetery chapel, speak- ing of the beautiful character and the honourable came - of the deenised, and as far as was possible he had en- deavoured to further the work of his Master, the Lore. There was a large attendance at the graveside the chief mourners being: Mrs. Wigfleld (widow), dr. W. W. Wigfield (son), MissU Gertrude and Miss Hilda Wigfield (daughters), Mr. and Mrs. Charles Mitchell (lion-in-law and daughter), Messrs. Charles E. Parker (Penketb) and Robert H. Posnett, Runcorn (nephews) Mr. jawes Dodds Mr. and Mrs. Tom Gandy, Mr. J. G Withirelaw, Mrs. Gunnell, Mr. J. T. Ambler, Mr. Israel Hampsonand Isaac Lloyd, all of Penketh ; Mr. David Garnett (Southport), Dr. Coates and Mr. Walton. A Dumber of floral tributes were sent by the widow, son, Gertrude and Hilda, Mr. and Mrs. Charles Mitchell, Mr. rnd Mrs. T. Walker, Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker, Ml: Edward Walker and family, Mrs. James Walker, Mr.and Mrs. Ga"dy, Mrs. Coop, Chesterfield; Mr. and Mrs. W. Coates, " Ellen" and "all at Newhall Grange," and one from the employes of the Penketh Tanning Co., me</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S COUGHS. • MRS. BALLIN, THE GREAT AUTHORITY ON CHILDREN'S DISEASES, STRONGLY RECOMMENDS VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. Mrs. ADA S. BALLIN, 5, Agar-street, Loudon, Editor of "Womanhood," writes : VENO'S LIG WINING COUGH CURE is an exceedingly successful remedy. It is very pleasant to tske, and the relief it gives is vett, rapid. The preparation is perfectly safe for children.' Mrs. HAGUE, Ratcliffe Crescent, Lockwood, near Huddersfield, writes :—"My little boy suffered from hoarseness and bronchitis. We all thought he never would 'be cured or speak plain again. After taking VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE he was able to speak plainly. I have never seen anything to act so quickly." Ask for VENOS LIGHTNING COUGH CURE at Chemists and Drug Stores, Price 90., is. lid. and 2s. 9d. Sold everywhere.</p>
        <p>WIDNES MAN KILLED AT ST. HELENS. The inquest on John Tandy, engine fireman, aged thirty-one, of 78, Victoria-street, Widnes' who was cut to pieces by a passenger train betwcm St. Helens and Peasley Cross on Monday evening, was held at St. Helens Town Hall on Tuesday night, by Mr. Brighouse. —The Coroner said the company attached no blame to deceased. He was on a goods train working from Widnes to St. Helens. and left the engine to go to the Broad Oak signal-box to give the signalman information as to the places where the waggons bad to go, when he was knocked down. A goods train that was passing no doubt drew his attention, and caused him to neglect to pay sufficient attention to the passenger train. The heel of his boot was afterwards found jammed between the check rail and the rail on which the train ran.—A juryman questioned the wisdom of a man leaving his engine to take a mersage to the signal-box, but Mr. Davies, locomotive superintendent, read a rule under which be was directed to do this.—A verdict of" Acci- dental death " was recorded.</p>
        <p>PROMINENT FOOTBALL OFFICIAL'S WIFE DROWNED. A sensation was occasioned at St. Helens on Monday, when it became known that the wife of a highly respected townsman had been found drowned in the ornamental lake in Victoria Park, Cowley Hill. During the forenoon a Corporation workman saw on one of the seats a lady's cape, hat and gloves. Later, the body of a respectably-dressed lady was dragged to the side, and it was identified as that of Louisa Glimmer, 39, of Den- ton's Green. She was the wife of Mr. Thomas Garner, cashier for Messrs. Nuttall and Company, end chairman of the etommittee of the St. Helens Northern Union Footballl Club and of the Windle Bowling Club. Mrs. Garner, who had not been well for some time, left the house early in the morning.</p>
        <p>Two difficult slow-screw in-offs.—Position A, red ball of an inch from the side cushibn and 10 inches from the top cushion. Cue ball 50 inches from the side cushion and four inches from the top cushion. Position B, red ball touching the side cushion, but all other measurements the same az at A.</p>
        <p>Diagram 95 shows two slow-screw in-offs into tomer pockets. In both cases 'check side (left Land side in position A and right hand aide in position B) must be •used. owing to the pocket in each case being very blind. The in-off at must be played with quite gentle strength in order to keep the object ball from going into baulk. The stroke is therefore a very difficult one for all but first class players. It might be thought by many moderate players that 'the safer game would be to play this in-off with sufficient strength to drive the object ball in and out of baulk and thus not risk the chance of the ball just going over the line as it May do when playing, to keep it out of baulk. In order, how- 'ever, to drive the object ball in and out of baulk a great deal of strength would have to be used, ifor before the ball could come out of baulk it would have to strike the baulk side and baulk cushion, and traverse a good deal of, the baulk enclosure. It may not seem that very much strength would be necessary for this, but how much is rewiired mAy be easily ascertained by simply :playing on to the object ball without screw, taking care to drive it in the same direc- tion that it Would have to travel were the screw sn-off' being played for. A trial of this would surprise many. The object ball in such a stroke always comes off the baulk side cushion, in a very dead manner, in fact it strikes this cushion syith check side. This check side off the baulk side cushion is the result of the side which has been imparted to the cue ball, through its having been pressed into the cushion close to which it was lying previous to being struck. As explained in the chapter on "Side." this is cushion imparted side and has nothing whatever to do with transmitted side, in fact the side imparted io an object ball by a cushion when a fast ball strikes it at an angle is always the smile whether</p>
        <p>The cue ball has been struck with running side cd. with cheek side.</p>
        <p>Played with sufficient strength to drive the object ball in and out of baulk the screw in-off vill always fail; in fact anything like a strong stioke is almost certain to prevent the in-off being made. With the object ball so near the side cushion, it is clearly evident that the cue- ball must recoil from the object ball in a straight line, or very nearly a straight line to the pocket in order to enter it. In a previous article I explained that whenever the cue-ball is near the object ball, and a strong screw stroke is played, the cue ball as it recoils always de- scribes a curve (unlese the object ball has been hit quite full). In the shot under discussion a strong screw prevents the ball from recoiling in a straight line, instead, if the same aim has been taken as far a slow screw, the cue ball recoils on to the cushian and then rebounds right away. This different direction or recoil, when the stroke is played strongly is caused by the cue-ball's curve already referred to, though the contact with the cushion prevents the curve being xtoticP able.</p>
        <p>There is another reason that may also conduce to the failure of the in-off when plenty of strength is used. When the cue and object ball are near to each other, a very strong screw-back stroke has a tendency to drive the cue ball some slight distance past the point of con- tact, more especially if the butt of the cue be raised, and of course when the cue-ball is near a cushion the butt of the cue must be somewhat raised in order to be able to hit the ball below the centre. When a cue ball, with a rotation which is a kind of mean between pure reverse rotation and side rotation (caused by hitting a ball below the centre and with side) is forced through another ball, it never recoils in a straight line. For every reason then, the screw in-off at A: should be played gently enough to keep the object ball out of baulk. The stroke is not an easy one, but should not be beyond the player who makes occasional fifty breaks. The screw in-off shown at B though of a sirni. lar nature to the one at A, is still more difficult. It is played in exactly the same way as the screw, at A, but owing to the object ball being dead on the cushion, extremely exact hitting is required. Should the object ball be hit just the least bit too full the cue ball will recoil from it in a direction away from the cushion, and on the other hand, should the contact not be full enough by the merest margin, the cue, bail will strike the cushion immediately after hitting the object ball and the stroke will fail. In fact the object ball must be hit to a nicety in order for the in-off to be made, and such a &amp;Take is therefore best left alone by all but really good players. In discussing the screw n-off shown at A on Diagram 95, I referred to the posseibility cC the cue ball going beyond the point of contaell before coming back should the stroke be attempted with great strength. That the cue ball may travel a very consideraole distance beyond its point of contact with an object ball and then come back, may be shown most con. vincingly by any player who has plenty of power of cue. If a ball be placed on the baulk line and another ball eight or nine inches directly behind it as shown on Diagram 96 and a plain scrim.</p>
      </div>
