      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases seal their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>Councillor Stott and the Next Election. Sir,—" Fairplay's " little juggling with the word " ignore" might have been effective if it had not been so clurnsilyadone. However, it may serve to evade the facts of the controversy ; but it is such a graceless movement that one cannot help smiling that he should find himself in such a silly and unlooked for entangle- ment. He makes unto himself a kind of " puzzle garden," loses himself in it, and then cautiously invites some kindly passer by to show him how he got there so that he may extricate himself without being actually led. HI had charged Councillor Stott with having used the word " ignore " his amiment might possibly have been effective, but, for obvious reasons, I didn't. I have written more than I intended at first to write. Whether Councillor Stott is justified in taking up the position he has will be left for the public to decide— the public, whose peculiar province it is, and who are far more likely to come to a decision on the subject than are " Fairplay " and "NUNQUAL3I." P.S.—" Fairplay " will pardon me, I am sure, for not entering into a long criticism of his ideals. I am a Socialist myself, and believe that the future has many good things in store for us if we will work more—and talk less. N.</p>
        <p>Ist "%ray, 1905. [Correspondence on this subject must now cease.— ED." Exn</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Dunnico and The Sodall Gospel of Jesus. Sir,—Before entering into the controversial arena I would first express my sincere regret on learning that the Rev. H. Dunnico's health has for some time been failing and I hope, as I am sure all of us do, that he will speedily regain that health and strength which gives one a broader and more sympathetic view of life and men. In last Sunday's sermon, according to newspaper reports, the rev, gentleman gave utterance to one or two statements which, if not entirely false, are not the whole truth and which are undoubtedly misleading— though there may have been no intention to mislead. When a man says that preaching to-day is not at a dis- count he must be sitting under the ministry of an excep- tionally able and interesting preacher ; he cannot conceivably have sampled the generality of parsons. In the case before us however a preacher himself makes the statement and this is by far more surprising. To make the statement that the Gospel of Christ has lost none of its power to attract the masses of mankind must in one sense be granted, but in another labelled as untrue. The more educated and enlightened men grow, so in proportion there springs up an antipathy to the hell-fire doctrines of Christianity—of course this doctrine is shelved before intelligent audiences. Ithas however lost none of its power to fascinate the masses, and were it not for the masses the doctrines of Chris- tianity would be very soon buried amid the ruins of a great Church. What are really the doctrines of Chris- tianity nobody seems to have the slightest idea, and were Jesus, if He ever lived, to return to this fragment of the glorious sun it seems to me that He also would feel a strange dizziness at the task of rescuing the remains of a forgotten past. Then again in the middle of the sermon under criticism there is mention of what is called the Social Gospel of Jesus. This is spoken of as though Jesus, if He had a Social Gospel, is the originator of all moral teaching and the only guide in life. And if we are to believe our spiritual pastors all that Christ did teach was a social Gospel. If Christ were here to-day, we are informed, He would say that men ought to have regular work and that no honest people should ever be hungry. Wonder- ful ! But who would say otherwise? He would also say that masters should pay a living wage, and that men should give an honest day's work in return. Is there anything new or wonderful in this? Any fool could say that Christ would say this and say the other; let the rev. gentleman speak for himself, for the teachings and words ascribed to Christ do not suggest any extraordinary intelligence and certainly do not indicate a capacity to grasp and solve great social problems. And one need not mention also the very grave doubt as to whether there are any authentic teachings or words of Jesus in order to throw further discredit upon the claims of Jesus as an original and supreme social reformer. What contribution has Jesus made to the question of -master and workman? Does Jesus hold (or did he hold) that the capitalist class is necessary in the industrial system. Did Jesus teach that land monopoly (and other monopolies) were just and fair ? Where in any place has Jesus made a specific contribution to any great question? A few moral precepts and parables are all that can be produced and most of these if not all can be nearly paralelled in the religions existent prior to Christianity. What for instance, has Jesus to say on the Darwinian theory ? Did he know anything about it? Has Jesus ever made any practical suggestions towards the settling of the housing problem and the brain puzzling elusive fiscal question ? A good many ministers select a text to preach from and expoend their own pet theories and try to make the text apply to every ques- tion under the sun,and doing this they solemnly inform us that Christ taught all they have indicated and out of the echoes of the night the voice of the dreamer speaks and asks in tones of wonder and surprise—where? Until some better proof than the empty vapourings of parsons, whose minds are not free, is forthcoming Jesus must rank along with other men who have lived, taught and died a natural death. Not like the famous jester who asked "What is truth?" I humbly beg to ask ‘ What is the soul ?"—Sincerely yours W. 11. BELL, Jun. 38, Bewsey street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PROSECUTION OF A PENKETH PUBLICAN.</head>
        <p>PROSECUTION OF A PENKETH PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>Case Dismissed.</p>
        <p>George Davies, landlord of the Red Lion Inn, Penketh, appeared at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday in answer to a charge of per- mitting drunkenness on his licensed premises on the 22nd April. After a fairly lengthy hearing the magis- trates, Messrs. J. White and .1. J. Bleckley, dismissed the charge holding that sufficient evidence had not been produced to justify them in convicting. P.C. Houghton stated that at 7.40 on the night in question he visited the Red Lion Inn and there found a man named Frederick Thompson, who was drunk, drink- ing from a glass containing beer. He called the land- lord's attention to the man and Davies said he had not served him. It was afterwards found that Thompson 1144:1 been served by a barmaid named Alice Harrison. Th 9 landlord afterwards put Thompson out. Mr. Browne, who appeared for the defendant: Was not Thompson summoned here eighteen months ago, and have you not had it in for him ever since? Wanes: No. Mr. Browne : Was not Thompson persuading another man named Rotherham to go home? Witness: No. Further questioned,he said there were 12 or 14 -nen in the bar at the time. He did not see Thompson light his cigarette and walk out. Thompson staggered when walking in the road. Mr. Browne : Did you see Thompson actually stand- ing outside the inn talking to some other people? Witness : He went down the road and was a general nuisance to the village. Mr. Browne : Did you not ask a man named Purcell to give evidence? Witness : He said he would not come as he said there</p>
