      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>GASSING FATALITY. BRAVERY FITTINGLY RECOGNIZED. Presentation of Royal Humane Society</head>
        <p>THE GASSING FATALITY. BRAVERY FITTINGLY RECOGNIZED. Presentation of Royal Humane Society</p>
        <p>Medal and. Certificates.</p>
        <p>The distressing gassing fatality which °mired in Ellesmere-street on March 6th, is still fresh in the minds of Warrington people. It will be remembered that on that date James Donoghue, a Corporation em- ploy&amp; was working in a sewer when he was overcome by gas fumes. A fellow workman, Richard Bretherton at once went to his assistance and was in turn over- come. A similar fate betel two other Corporation em- ployees, John Mitchell and James Baxter, who in turn entered the sewei. A carter, John Stockton then appeared on the scene and at great personal risk entered the sewer and after a second attempt, and aided by the men in the roadway he succeeded in extricating the men from their dangerous position. In the case of Baxter his servic-s were unfortunately too late, Baxter dying a few minutes after being brought to the surface. These facts were at once communicated to the Royal Humane Society, who in the case of each man fittingly recognized the bravery which was dis- played. Their awards were distributed by the Mayor lat the Coroner's Court on Wednesday, Alderman Bolton being accompanied by the Mayoress, Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, Alderman 'hellion, Mr. H. Roberts, Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (Town Clerk), and Mr. T. Longdin (Borough Surveyor). In making the presentations the Mtvon said he had to perform a duty which was at the same time pleasant and yet sad. It was pleasant to assist in the recog- nizing of bravery, yet at the same time he would probably awaken memories which some would rather forget. His Worship then briefly detailed the facts of the accident as stated above and announced the awards which had been made by the Royal Humane Society. To Mrs. Baxter he would hand the Society's "In Memoriam" on vellum and mounted; to Bretherton and Mitchell he would present certificates of bravery on vellum; and to Stockton he had to hand the Society's silver medal and a mounted certificate on vellum. (Applause). Mr. BRIGHOUSE supplimented the remarks of the Mayor. He had not had the sorrowful task of enquir- ing into the death of Baxter. That unpleasant duty had fallen to his deputy, Mr. F. A. Jones, but he had had the opportunity of reading the depositions taken at the inquest. Stockton was a person upon whom there rested no legal or moral obligation to do anything. Yet in spite of this, and in spite of the fact that he had only just recovered from a severe illness, he at great risk to his own life, succeeded in saving the lives of two others. We read the history of deeds of heroism at sea and on the battle-field, and we were always pleased to think those who did the deeds were akin to us. In the under walks of life also deeds of heroism were performed, and he ventured to say that the deed performed by John Stockton in risking his own life to save the lives of others equalled in bravery any of those which we read about. (Hear, hear,) Stockton was to receive a small token of regard from the Royal Humane Society and he could assure Stockton they all had an equal regard for him, and would be pleased to shake him by the hand, in the hope that if the time came they would be able to do their duty as he had done his. (Applause.) The MAYOR in handing the" In Memoriam "to Mrs. Baxter, said he hoped she would value it and that it would ever remind her of the bravery of her husband. He had known Baxter personally and had always found him to be a man ready to do his duty. (Heaa,hear.) The Mayor complimented Mitchell on the noble manner in which he had done his part in attempting to save his fellow workmen and also complimented Bretherton on the bravery he had shown. He was proud to think Stockton was a fellow townsman, and must express his admiration of his heroic conduct. He had been asked by Mitchell and Bretherton to tendert° him their heart- felt thanks. On behalf of the widow and children of the man who lost his life he also tendered their thanks. Though he was unable to save Baxter's life they none the less appreciated his bravery. He also deserved to thank Stockton on behalf of his fellow townsmen, in whose hearts he had by his bravery won a warm place. He had the grGatest pletsure in presenting him with the medal and certificate of the Royal Humane Society and with the medal and certificate of the Liverpool Ship- wreck and Humane Society. On behalf of the Corpora- tion he had also pleasure in handing him a ,ClO note in recognition of his bravery, aad it afforded him the greatest pleasure to shake hands with him and to think that he was 3 Warringtonian and a Britisher. (Applause.) The Mxvou explained that the certificates were all signed by H.R.H. the Prince of Wales, and generously offered to have them suitably framed—an offer which was in each case accepted. Mr. F. J. WHITTLE proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor for presenting the awards and to the Mayoress for her presence. He greatly deplored the accident which had happened but at the same time they could congratulate themselves that they had amongst them heroic' men who were ready to sacrifice themselves for their fellow men. Mr. CHALMERS seconded. In putting the resolution to the meeting Mr. H, Roberts said he cordially endorsed everything which had been said. They were especially grateful for the circumstances which had been again disclosed with re- gard to the great personal bravery of the men, which had that morning received fitting recognition, and more especially of the part played by the man who lost his life in an attempt—unfortunately a fruitless but nevertheless a brave attempt to save the lives of others. if there was any satisfaction, and it must be a great deal of satisfaction to the widow and children it was to know in parting with the head of the household, that he had exemplified what was the noblest element in humanity. We were told that "he who lays down his life for that of a friend discloses the highest form of Christian charity," and the widow and family of the late Mr. Baxter had the right to take that as the motto for their household. He desired in conclusion,to most cordially express his gratitude for the fact that we had amongst im a man, who though in a humble walk in life, had shed great lustre upon the good name of Warrington. (Applause.) The vote was heartily accorded and a brief response by the Mayor concluded the proceedings.</p>
        <p>EASTER VESTRY MEETING. CHRIST CHURCH, LATCHFORD. The Easter vestry meeting in connection with Christ Church, Latchford, was held on Saturday evening the vicar (the Rev. G. C. Briggs) presiding over a small attendance. FINANCIAL. The statement of accounts, which was approved, showed a balance due to the wardens of £9 Is. 53d. The special offertories amounted to £lO9 10s. 10d., and the general offertory to £l6O 16s. sd. The repair fund came to £25, and the Easter offertory to £53 ss. 33d. On the expenditure side one-third of the general offertory, namely, £53 its. 2d., had been given to the Vicar. The repairs to the steeple and pointing outside the churce cost £l9 Is. and the decoration fund took 250. The Vicar said he considered the accounts very satis- factory when they remembered the extraordinary expenses which they had had to face during the year. (Hear, hear.) APPOINTNIFNT OF OFFICERS. The Vicau re-appointed Mr. John Fairclough as his warden and on the motion of Mr. Taylor, seconded by Mr. Kirkham, Mr. W. Longbottom was re-elected people's wsrden. Mr. PAIRCLOUGII nominated Mr. Bernard as his sides man, and Mr. Longbottom selected Mr. R. T. Fairclough The other sidesmen appointed were Messrs. T. 1-1. Plevin, John Fairclough (ktintsford-road), J. Lowe, W. J. Forshaw, George Dakin, William Tinsle I. T,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 6, 1905.</head>
