      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WARRINGTON RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>REVIEW OF THE SEASON 1904-5.</p>
        <p>A Great Year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>By " Oyalist."</head>
        <p>By " Oyalist."</p>
        <p>A year ago I preceded my annual review with the remark that some day I should take up my pen with , pleasure and alacrity for the task—when Warrington had won the Cup! I hardly imagined, however, that Warrington would make a third appearance in the final exactly a year later and carry off the coveted trophy. At Salford they seemed to miss the chance of a lifetime, and a weary wait for several years wottld not have been surprising. But Dame Fortune has at last been kind and the Warrington men are now supremely happy in possession of the Northern Union Cup. It is rather a curious coincidence that a year ago the" Yorkshire Post " commented on , Warrington's weakness forward and expressed the opinion that with another half-stone a man in the scrummage and the same set of backs they would win the cup. If that half-stone per man has not been ob- tained to the pound, the forwards have been very greatly strengthened by the inclusion of Preston, Shugars and Belton, and the result is that Warrington have not only secured the cup, but they have had far and away their best season under the Northern Union. It is hardly too much to say that, at present, the War- rington fifteen is probably the strongest in the Union, while the reserve men, by carrying off the Combina- tion Championship, give solid testimony to/the strength of the Warrington club's 'resources. In League matches Warrington have been remarkably successful, and it is certain that if they had laid them- selves out for a sustained effort they would have had to be seriously reckoned with for the League champion- ship. The latter has gone to Oldham with 51 points, while Warrington come fifth with 42 points—their record, and an improvement of 5 points on the score a year ago. The number of wins is 20 out of 34 matches, an excellent proportion, while the point aver- age is also the best in the Club's League history. Warrington started the season in fine style by visiting Leigh and inflicting a thrashing of 16 points to 2 points on the ,lather-laners. Most of the supporters of the 1 Club will recollect how marked was the improvement in forward play consequent on , the introduction of the men mentioned above, and high hopes were at once entertained. These were somewhat dashed the next fortnight when Warrington could only draw at home with Halifax —their Cup-final foes—and succumbed at Hull by the margin of a point. The last match in September, however, restored confidence for Bradford were "taken in and done fgt. "to the tune of 13 points to nil. Two reserve half packs, in E. Brooks and J. Davies, distinguished themselves that day, and both have •since proved themselves very valuable members of the Club. In October, November, and December, the team's fortunes varied, but a respectable level of football was maintained until the last match of the year when a poor exhibition both of football and refereeing resulted in the first home defeat. Leigh being the successful team. The ' New Year, with its abundance of home matches, started promisingly for Halifax (away) and Hull (home) both afforded Warrington sweet revenge. Runcorn were beaten for the secand time, the match being memorable for the accident to Hockenhall,which kept him out of the team for practically the whole of the remainder of the season. On successive Satur- day's Warrington were humbled, for Oldham won at Wilderspool on February 11th, and Wigan trounced the Wirepullers at Wigan on the 18th by 16 points to 8. Thence forward Warrington asserted themselves and from that date to this they have only sustained ; two defeats-Batley and Wakefield away—a run of success which has not been equalled in the club's history for many a long year, and which culminated in the capture of the Cup. The struggle for the latter is so recent as to need no description here, but I will just add my congratulations to the Warrington team on their success at the third attempt. The most interesting feature of the Cup tourney was the defeat of Bradford in the semi-final for the second time by I Warrington. By now the Park Avenue brigade should have a wholesome respect for Hallam's men. By the way, Hallam has proved himself a worthy skipper, though at the commencement of the season the cares I of Captaincy appeared to weigh heavily: upon him. , • With regard to the players, Captain Hallam has, this season, proved himself the most capable custodian in Lancashire, and his form in the latter half of the season can only be described as magnificent. Fish is as tricky as ever, while Isherwood and Dickinson are both playing well. The backs in fact have had a good season, with the exception of Hockenhall and Harris, both of whom have suffered from injury. Their loss has, however, been the gain of Brooks and Kenyon, two young players whose future is bright with promise. The forwards have also done well, and the first eight are now a capable and powerful lot, though there is still room for improvement. Quite the most valuable addition to the team has been" Jack " Preston, the Lancashire County forward, whose field- work and fine goal-kicking have made him one of the idols of the crowd. Coming to the detailed particulars I find that during the season Warrington have pla-ed 41 matches, of which they have won 26, lost 12, and drawn 3. They have scored 49 goals, 63 tries or 287 points, against 32 goals 31 tries,or 157 points. Two of their draws were pointless, and in addition to these they have " nilled " their opponents 15 times. Their defence has, as a matter of fact, been about the best in the Northern Union. On only four occasions have they been " nilled " them- selves, when the game was lost, the four teams to accomplish this being Salford, Batley, Wakefield Trinity, and Hull Kingston Rovers. No Yorkshire Club has won at Warrington this season, while the only Club to take four points out of Warrington is Oldham— the League Champions. NVarrington have beaten the following clubs twice: Bradford, Runcorn, Wigan, Swinton, Hunslet, Widnes and Hull Kingston Rovers. The following clubs have been beaten once each :—Leigh, Halifax, Hull, Leeds, Batley, Salford, Broughton Rangers, Wakefield Trinity, St. Helens, Cheshire, Morecambe and Keighley. Warring- ton have drawn with St. Helens, Halifax and Keighley. Warrington have lost once to Leigh, Hull, Bradford, Leeds, Wigan, Hull Kingston Rovers, Batley, Salford, Broughton Rangers and Wakefield Trinity. The scoring particulars of each match during the season are as follows : Points. 1901. For agst. Sept. 3 Leigh away 16 2 lifax</p>
        <p>~ „ 10 Halifax home 0 0 „ 17 Hull 4 away 7 8 24 Bradford Oct. 1 Leeds away 311 „ 8 Runcorn „ 15 Oldham away 6 8 ~ 22 Wigan „ 29 Swinton away 11 0 Nov. 12 Hull Kingston R. „ 19 Batley „ 25 Hunslet away 7 6 Dec. 3 Widnes home 9 0 „ 10 Salford „ 17 Broughton Rangers away 3 9 „ 21 Wakedold Trinity „ 25 St. Helens __,._ 31 Leigh home 2 5</p>
        <p>. _ 19.05. Jan. 7 Halifax „ 14 Hull „ 21 Bradford „ 28 Leeds home 6 0 Feb. 4 Runcorn away 5 3 „ 11 Oldham home 710 „ 18 Wigan away 816 „ 25 Swinton Mar. 4 Morecombe Cup Tie home 30 0 „ 7 Cheshire County home 5 0 „ 11 Hull Kingston R home 4 2 „ 18 Keighley Cup Tie away 3 3 „ 21 Keighley re-played away 7 0 „ 25 Hunslet home 8 7 „ 28 Batley away 0 7 April 1 Wigan Cup Tie „ 8 Salford home 5 0 „ 10 Widnes „ 15 Bradford semi-Rnal at Whdale 7 2 „ 17 Broughton Rangers home 19 3 „ 21 St. llelens away 5 0 „ 22 Wakefield Trinity „ 23 Hull Kingston R. Final at Leeds o 0</p>
        <p>Fish again heads the list of individual scorers, while Preston makes a good second. The value of these two men is amply proved by the figures. The scorers are : Goals. Tries. Points. Fish Preston 2O ... 2 ~. 40 Dai Davies Harris Isherwood Berry 4 2 ... 14 Dickenson Brookes Kenyon Hockenhall Boardman — ... 3 ... 9 Thomas Hallam</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>VIVISECTION CONDEMNED.</head>
        <p>VIVISECTION CONDEMNED.</p>
        <p>Interesting Address at Warrington. The practice of vivisection was strongly condemned in an address delivered in Cairo-street schools, Warring- ton, on Tuesday afternoon, by Miss Lind (one of the Swedish ladies who took part in the Bayliss-Coleridge case). The meeting was held under the auspices of the Warrington Anti-vivisection Society and was Largely attended, Dr. Joseph presiding. In the course of a short address, the CHAIRMAN said Miss Lind had done and was doing noble service in the cause of the poor, dumb creatures which suffered at the hands of vivisectionists. That one of his calling should take a prominent part in the movement to oppose a practice which physiologists claimed was a valuable aid to the advancement of knowledge, was for the present pretty generally looked upon with surprise, but he was perfectly consistent in his action, as he had all along been dead against i any of these experiments. He had i never taken part n vivisection, and in fact he had never been presentat a vivisection operation. After pro- longed experience n the pursuit of his calling, during the whole of which time he had been connected with hospitals and with the surgical and medical work there, he could hardly call to mind a single instance where help was required from experiments of vivisection in order to promote the cure of patients. (Heir, hear.) In con- clusion, he would remind them that the history of great discoveries in medicine or allied sciences had generally been accidental and very often had been made by persons humble and obscure, and not by Royal Commissions of inquiry or even by the so-called noble band of scientific vivisectors. Applause.) Miss LIND dealt very ably and interestingly with her subject. At one time, she said, she did not believe that vivisection was as bad as anti-vivisectionists described it. She therefore decided to take up the study of phy- siology, to go into the laboratories, and to be present at vivisectional demonstrations to see whether there was any cruelty or whether any good to physiology or the art of healing could be derived from tho experi- ments and she saw about one hundred demonstrations. Miss Lind pointed out that a student could not take a physiological degree without vivisecting, and every pressure was put on young students to draw them into that kind of research. The result was that i physiological study had developed n the technique of the most lciathsome, vile experiments on animals. Miss Lind described a number of operations she had witnessed and contended that much vivisecting was done out of the mere curiosity. It was absolutely impossible to learn any true physiology by means of vivisection. During the operations the organs were in an abnormal condition, owing to the administration of amesthetics, and the frightened state of the animals before the operations. The means used in vivisection could really compete with the means used by the butcher in the slaughter-house, and were as far rornoved Irons any true scientific method as could be imagined. Did they find that vivisectors agreed ? No. As the result of their work there was one vast field of contra- dictions and ever-changing theories. That was simply because Nature broughtthe punishment for the violation of her laws by teaching us absolutely nothing but amass of confused theories which were created to-day and gone to-morrow. The effect on the students of increasing vivisection experiments could be nothing but debasing Some of the stadents hated the experiments and would quickly leave the operating rooms if they were not afraid of being called sentimental. The majority of experi- ments were of the inoculation character, and people believed they were going to find a great remedy for human ills by cruelly treating animals. One point which decided some people in favour of vivisection was the discovery of the anti-toxine treatment of diphtheria. If the researches of vivisectors had been directed to remedying that disease, was it not a terrible thing that diphtheria should have increased by leaps and bounds since vivisectors commenced their work? The anti-vivisection movement was started wholly from the moral point of view. They must now try to meet the vivisectors on their own ground and prove to them that if a thing was morally wrong it could not be right on the scientific or acv other plane and could not lead to any good. 11.1iss Lind commended the work of the new International Medical Anti- vivisection Association, and expressed the hope that the organization would be able to offer prizes to students for painless physiological research. The whole practice of vivisection was apt to make people callous. If it were allowed to go on, there would be a degeneration in science and morals, and they could not contemplate the ultimate consequences. Personally she believed vivi- section would die a natural death in science but they must hasten that death. (Applause.) The CIIA.IRMN remarked that the only debatable point, to his mind, was the serum question. His ex- perience might, he thought, prove of use in bringing WObblerB " to a decision. The serum treatment all round had not been encouraging to those who had sup- ported it. He had seen bad cases of tetanus recover years ago before the anti-toxine treatment was known. Ile had seen one or two cases recover with the anti- toxine treatment, but it had alway been coupled with the old treatment. With regard to diphtheria, a very significant fact was that even now a fresh mode of treat- ment was being looked for. If the serum treatment i were as good as t was sought to make out they would not be looking for fresh curative agents. On the motion of Mr. NV. H. J. JENKINS, seconded by the Rey. J.F.MATTHEWS a vote of thanks was accorded to Miss Lind and to the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WARRINGTON WIN THE CUP.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WIN THE CUP.</p>
        <p>"THIRD TIME PAYS FOR ALL."</p>
        <p>A Fine Victory at Leeds.</p>
        <p>(By " Ovallst."i</p>
        <p>It was a confident crowd which left Warrington for Leeds, where the final for the Northern Union Cup was to be decided, Warrington and Huh King4ton Rovers being the contesting clubs. By Saturday, the disappointment at Preston's absence from the team had been got used to by both players and supporters and it was felt that, given average luck, the Warrington men must win. The number of people who followed the team to Leeds, probably constituted, a record for Warrington, and the ten special trains could not have taken less than 5,000. Both the London and . North-Western and the Midland Railway Cos. this time made special efforts to ensure the punctual running of the' trains, and the big crowd was con- veyed to Leeds n record time. En route, the weather had been variable, with perhaps sunshine predominat- ing but at Leeds the outlook was leaden and the streets wet. Frequent showers fell, but nothing could damp the enthusiasm of the Warrington and Hull supporters who appeared to comprise the bulk of the population in the streets. Club colours were strongly in evidence, the Warrington men quite taking the honours with the ingenuity of their patriotic devices. There were quite .half-a-dozen primrose and blue umbrellas, while the number of copper "fishes " as button-holes was tremendous. One well-known supporter of the Warrington team was resplendent in . a bowler hat, completely covered with primrose and I blue panels. Long before the time for the kick-off the ' crowd began to make its way to Headingley and at 3 o'clock there was a fine concourse of spectators, while the number was rapidly increasing. Ultimately the ground was well filled, and a crowd of 19;468 must be regarded as very, satisfactory considering the weather, and the fact that the competing teams were foreign to the district. The receipts totalled £1,261 3s. Other finals have realised considerably more, as the following statistics show. • Where Played. Attdce. Rcpts. 1897...Headingley...Batley v. St. Helens 13,492 £624 1898...Hoadingley...Batley v. Bradford 27,941. £1,586 1899...Manchester—Oldham v. Hunslet 15,763 '1946 1900...Manchester...Swinton v. Salford 17,864 £3,110 1901...Headingley...Batley v. Warrington 29,569 £1,644 1902...Rochdale Broughton Rv. Salford 15,000 £846 1903...Headingley Halifax v. Salford 32,507 £1,834 1904...5a1f0rd Halifax v. Warrington 17,041 £938</p>
        <p>There was a last minute change in both teams, War- rington playing Harmer in preference to Morrison for- ward, while D. Reed took the place of Townsley in the.Rovers' pack. In other respects the Hull team was absolutely at full-strength. Warrington were, of course, lamenting the absence of Hockenhall (half back) and Preston, their best forward, through injuries, but the team was nevertheless representative. The teams and officials were : WARRINGTON. Height. Age. FL in.</p>
        <p>Weight. St. lbs.</p>
        <p>J. Hallam (capt.) 26 5 81 11 8 Three-quarter backs: J. Fish 25 5 74 .1110 D. Isherwood G. Dickenson T. Kenyon 2l 5 7 - 11 0 Half backs :</p>
        <p>•D. Davies E. Brooks Forwards:</p>
        <p>_ A. E. Boardman 23 5 10i 14 4 G. Thomas 23 5 81 12 11 F. G. Shugars G. Jolley IV. Belton ) 26 6 11 12 12 A. Naylor W. Swift 24 5 11 12 0 Harmer HULL KINGSTON ROVERS. Height. Weight. Back: Age. Ft. In. St. lb. _._. . .</p>
        <p>•H. Sinclair 27 5 8:t 12 6 2 Three-quarter backs : W. Madley 25 5 7 11 6 A. W. Robinson W. Phipps 27 5 9 • 12 12 G. H. West 23 5 41 10 0 Half-backs:</p>
        <p>J. Barry J. Gordon 3O 5 5 10 10 Forwards:</p>
        <p>A—Siaiks (capt.) 32 6 0 12 10 A. Kemp 2B 5 111 13 9 W. Osborne A. Spackman 26 5 9 14 0 A. Windle G. Ellis F. Gorman 32 5 11 14 3 D. Reed 23 511 13 0</p>
        <p>Referee: Mr. J. Bruckshaw, Stockport; Touch- judges, Messrs. J. H. Smith (Widnes) and W. Robinson, Bradford</p>
        <p>Prompily to time the teams appeared, and both met with a great reception, that which greeted the Warring- ton men being certainly the more hearty. As finalists for the third time they had the popular sympathy and good wishes. The weather had now considerably improved, but the ground was very heavy and holding. The Rovers won the toss, and Dai Davies set the ball in motion for Warrington. His initial kick however, was badly screwed and failed to cross the ten yards line, a scrummage following at centre. The War- rington forwards immediately displayed great activity andiThomas headed a rush into the Rovers' quarters. Here Davies was penalised at the scrummage and Sin- clair kicked strongly to centre with the aid of a fair breeze. Barry secured and tested Hallam with a smart punt. The Warrington custodian, cool as ever, fielded cleverly and returned the ball with interest by a fine left-leg punt. Warrington scrummaged with in opponents half again and Brooks passed out to Dicken- son, the latter making a big effort to bunt throught but being grassed by Robinson. So far, the expected superiority of the Rovers forwards had not been ap- parent, and the Warrington supporters were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ON -GOOD TERMS</head>
        <p>ON -GOOD TERMS</p>
        <p>with themselves. Now, however, Warrington had to fall back. Gordon, Phipps and West handled smartly, the right wing man getting away down the touch-line and ki,iking across, Harmer making a good save in the Warrington quarter. Short punting by Davies and smart following up changed the venue again, Sinclair falling a victim to Fish's pace. The Rovers again tried the passing game, and a long pass from Phipps to Robinson left Kenyon with a very awkward handful. Robinson was, hnwever, slow in gathering and Isherwood dashed across and brought him down finely. Dickinson was kominent with a strong run the next minute and the ball seemed likely to go across to Fish, but Isherwood was collared in the nick of time. So fat neither side had been very dangerous and the play was confined almost to midfield. Boardman, Brooks and Thomas put in a fast dribble which carried the game well into the Hull half. Here Barry was" too previous " and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>FISH TRIED AT GOAL</head>
        <p>FISH TRIED AT GOAL</p>
        <p>from the resulting penalty. He made a fine kick with the heavy ball, but it fell just under the bar. Warrington at this stage were very aggressive, Brooks being prominent, while Kenyon smartly returned a punt to the corner-flag. The IVar- rington forwards heeled out, but Davies was quickly collared. An attempt at relief was checked by Isher- wood who ran and punted well. Fish followed up, and the ball bouncing away from Sinclair was collared smartly by the Warrington crack. A score seemed eel., tam, but the muddy turf failed Fish who fell and appar- ently hur, his knee. There was.an anxious wait while the injured limb was doctored, and a cheer of relief when Fish limped back to his place. The Rovers had had a narrow escape, and a penalty kick came as a wel- come aid. Good work by Barry next tested the War- rington defence. He cleared smartly on the blind side of the scrum and ran well down touch. When challenged by Hallam he punted high and only Fish's expertness saved Warrington. He was compelled to carry the ball over the line, and a five yards scrummage followed. Cheering from the Rovers followers was now heard as the Warrington line became endangered. Davies secured,however, and kicked away. Sinclair in response tried a drop at goal, but was yards off the mark. Warrington could not clear out the invaders for West smartly ran and kicked into the goal-mouth again, and Brooks had to go down to save from the rush which followed.</p>
        <p>DESPERATE WORK on the Warrington line ensued, and the tackling was fierce all round. The Warrington defence was splendid, and the ball only got among the Rovers' threequarters once at this stage. West received the final pass and made for the line at his best pace. Fish met him half- way with a tremendous " chuck " which put the Hull star on his back, and the next minute the Warrington forwards relieved the strain by a rush to the 25 line. The excitement of the past few minutes had been intense and once thewhole of the barrier behind the goal leaned over with the pressure of the crowd, and a smash was narrowly averted. Before Warrington were clear Robinson had a fruitless drop at goal, and then Harmer dribbled smartly past the centre line. half-time sounded immediately afterwards with the score-sheet blank. Starks re-started with the odds certainly in favour of Warrington, for wind, shower, and sun had been against the Warrington men in the first half, and they had quite held their own. For a moment the Hull men made headway, until sent to the right-about by Fish who put in a huge kick which sent everybody on the run. Kenyon came across from the other side at a great pace and captured West near the line before he could get . the ball away. Warrington now began to show their</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>mettle. They forced the Rovers to the goal-mouth and secured the ball. Davies slung it out to ,Dickenson who transferred to Isherwood. The latter passed to Fish five yards from the line. Apparently there was no opening, but the "irrepressible" twisted out of the arms of West and Phipps and</p>
        <p>SCORED AT THE CORNER amid a tremendous outburst of cheers. The scorer took the goal kick but failed from a difficult position. It seemed Warrington's game now, and when from the drop-out Fish ran down and centred again the Rovers were hard pressed. Sinclair saved on the line, and from the next pack Spackman made a great dribble right to half way. Here a round of exchanges ended to the Rovers advantage, and a strong rally was made. Barry, Gordon, Phipps, and West handled speedily, and the wing man was faced by Fish. He attempted to dodge round but was speedily overwhelmed by the Warring- tonian. Play was in the ‘Varrington half when a re- markable change came over the scene.</p>
        <p>BROOKS BROKE AWAY on the left of the scrum and sprinted clear of the Hull threequarters. A roar of applause went up as Fish was seen in close attendance. Away the two sped down the field until Sinclair was reached. The young half back passed swiftly to Fish, who darted ahead and finished up a sensational movement with a try behind the posts. Hallam took the goal-kick but could only rattle the cross-bar with his shot. The game was as good as won, however, and the Warrington supporters were jubilant at the success of the team. Warrington completely overplayed the Rovers at all points and nearly all the play was now in the Dull quarters. Fish and Hallam both took shots at goal from marks and failed. The Rovers made one more effort which took play to the Warrington half. Phipps made a good run and passed to Robinson, who missed the ball. Warrington quickly rushed to the other end, Sinclair conceding a minor. The attack for a few minutes was very hot. Dickenson made a big effort which resulted in Medley's temporary retirement through injury, and Kenyon was collared by the leg only a yard from the line, after a gallant effort. On the other side there was a bout of quick passing by Brooks Isherwood, and Fish, the first-named running over, only to be re-called for a forward transfer. From now to the close, Warrington eased off somewhat, and contented themselves with keep- ing the Rovers on the defence. They were easily masters of the situation, and after Dickenson had had a fruitless shot at goal, the Warrington men ran out winners of the cup by 2 tries (6 points) to Hull Kings- ton Rovers, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Back:</head>
        <p>Back:</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF THE CUP.</p>
        <p>- - - Throughout the game, the massive silver challenge cup had been on view in the midst of a group of Northern Unior. officials, with their wives and friends, who were congregated immediately in front of the covered stand. For this point there was the usual stampede of spectators when the match ended, and the successful players had the greatest difficulty in forcing their way through the ranks of theif admiring sup- porters. Anthony Starks, the popular captain of the Rovers' team, made his way to the front, and there, in full view of the crowd, he very gallantly offered his hand and congratulations to each of the Warrington players in turn. It was a kindly and generous act, which was loudly applauded. Before introducing his wife to perform the presenta- tion ceremony, Mr. F. Lister, the Northern Union 'President, addressed a few words to the Warrington players, in which he heartily congratulated them on the good game they had played, and the success which had accrued to it. It was only at the third time of asking that they had been able to win the cup, and he was glad for their sakes that the object of their ambition had at last been attained. He congratulated Hallam on being the captain of the winning team, and offered his sym- pathy to Anthony Starks on having missed that honour, especially now that he was thinking of retiring from football. The attendance that day, he concluded, showed that the Northern Union was neither dead nor in a dying state.</p>
        <p>The-cuji was then formally banded by Mrs. Lister to Hallam, the Warrington captain, who passed it over to Anthony Starks. The latter drank to the health of the Warrington team, and then the cup was passed round. A vote of thanks to Mrs. Lister was adopted on the motion of Mr. J. B. Cooke, the president of the York- shire County Committee.</p>
        <p>DESCRIPTION OF THE CUP. There is no more valuable trophy offered for football competitions than the Northern Union Cup. Costing sixty guineas, the cup is a beautiful example of the silversmith's art. It stands about 36 inches in height, and rests on a black ebony base about eight inches deep. The medals are of 15 carat gold, and extremely neat, the design being both novel and pleasing. The obverse bears the inscription : Cancashire,Cheshire, Yorkshire, arranged on a graceful ribbon,beneath which is a golden football. Below, the arms of the respective counties are emblazoned in rich enamel,the correct heraldic tints being strictly adhered to. Surrounding the arms in raised characters are the words : "Northern Rugby Union Football Challenge Cup." The centre is occupied by an additional enamel in the form of a rose. The reverse is arranged to receive the title of the winning clubs and captains' names.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S HOME-COMING. Triumphal Procession Through Crowded Streets.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S HOME-COMING. Triumphal Procession Through Crowded Streets.</p>
        <p>MAGNIFICENT RECEPTION.</p>
        <p>Warrington streets rang with the cheers of thousands of overjoyed enthusiasts on Saturday night. The reception accorded to the victorious team on bringing theJong-coveted trophy to the good old town was in every respect worthy of a great occasion. The scenes will live long in the memories of the multitudes who witnessed them—and practically all Warrington joined in the carnival. The town was simply wild with delight. When the good news spread that the blue riband of Northern Union football was War- rington's for at least twelve months, the sole concern of the inhabitants was to do honour to the team who had emerged triumphant from the struggle. Even those whose interest in the sport is not very deep- rooted, were infected with the general excitement, and Saturday must have been the proudest night in the careers of Captain Hallam and his stalwart brigade. Early in the evening special trains from Leeds began to discharge their heavy freights of • • -</p>
        <p>a sea of faces, and the cheering was kept up almost continuously along the whole of the route. From Arpley the procession turned along Wilson Patten-street,, where the smaller crowd afforded a pleasant relief, and then made its way along Parker-street into Sankey- street. Here an immense concourse was waiting to. greet the conquerors. To add to the effectiveness of the picture Hallam and Fish stood up for a time, and supported the cup on their shoulders. In one by-street the spectators gave vent to their delight by discharging crackers,while fromlalmost every window along Sankey- street residents waved congratulations. At Market- gate a remarkable scene presented itself. On either side as far as the eye could reach spectators appeared to be massed in an impenetrable wall, and the enthusiasm reached its height. Down Buttermark et-street and Mersey street the vehicles proceeded amidst cheering lines, and in Mersey-street it was somewhat touching to notice that no one seemed to be more thoroughly delighted than an old white-haired man who joined in the welcome with a heartiness worthy of one more youthful. When Bridge-street was reached the thoroughfare looked impassable, for onlookers were assembled in thousands. Venturesome youths had taken possession of the Crom- well statue as being an elevated position from which to• view the rejoicings, and one clung tenaciously to the finger of the great Protector ! With policemen clearing the way, the triumphal march wa, resumed, Near Ryland-street the arrival of the team was signal- ised by the burning of coloured fires, and altogether the Spectacle baffled description. At one window an excited, female was seen throwing kisses to the team ! Along Horsemarket-street, Winwick-street, and Tanners'-lane the triumphal progress continued. The cup was the cynosure of all eyes, and Hallam and Fish never tired of holding it up for admiration. Thomas's jubilation was simply irrepressible. Whenever there was a lull in the cheering, he would raise the familar war-cry "Are we downhearted ? " and at once would come the long- drawn "No-o-o," given with a more earnest ring than ever. The band, too, never relaxed its energy, and dispensed popular music ranging in variety from "Rule Britannia to "The old Bull and Bush." Having traversed Frog- hall-lane,Crosfleld-street,a ndiSankey street the procession made a halt at the Town Hall where a large company had assembled on the steps. Short congratulatorkspeeches were delivered.</p>
        <p>Alderman HUTCHINSON, as a senior member of the Council present, was the first to offer words of welcome and his remarks were supported by Alderman SMETHURST.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said it was a good many years since he took an active interest in football, but he had, always been extremely proud of the town ; and when he knew that for the third time the team were in for the cup he could not resist the temptation to go and see the final struggle. It was a splendid fight and he could say without any disrespect to their opponents, who fought extremely well, that the best team had won. (Cheers.) He hoped that the spirit, pluck and perseverance which had been shown that day by the team in carrying off the prize would be shown by the citizens of Warrington in every good work and that they would try to excel in every direction as the team had excelled in the foot- ball field. Then they would have abundant reason to be proud of their citizens. He congratulated the club on the final triumph which had rewarded the labour they had expended for so many years. He hoped that now they had got the cup they would retain it. (Cheers.) Mr. NV. R. NEIL (the president of the club) said he thanked all present on behalf of the members and. officials for the kind manner in which the people had received the team that night; and he would also like to say how pleased he was that the cup had been brought to Warrington. They had been three times in the final and had at last succeeded. (Cheers). Every man had done well. They were not down-hearted when they started, and they were not down-hearted now. (Laughter and renewed cheers.) Captain HALLAM'S short speech was characteristic. " I am as proud as anybody to-night," he remarked "in fact, I think I would rather be a footballer than a Town Councillor "—a sally which provoked laughter and cheers. Continuing he said they had to thank the committee for the success which had been achieved. They had about the best committee in the Northern Union, and the players had always done their level best for them. On the other hand the committee had always done their level best for the players. (Hear, hear). He was also proud of Mr. Harry Ashton, who was one of the best known men in football circles in Lancashire or Yorkshire ; and he was sure that as long as he was connected with then) the Warrington Club would always do well. (Cheers)- " It has taken us three years to win the cup " said the popular captain in conclusion, "so we will try and keep it." (Cheers). The team then drove to the Bridge Inn where they had another great reception. Some enthusiasts re.-, mained in the streets, giving vent to their delight,:until early on Sunday niorning. WAMR. :CROSFIELD'S CONGRATULATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield wired as follows to Mr. T. Pemberton :—" Kindly convey heartiest congratulations to all members of team and committee on magnificent victory. The best side won, and by splendid play." Many telegrams and letters from private sources have also been received, and amongst others are those from Messrs. W. McCutcheon (Oldham), J. Clarke (Ruccorn), G. Underwood (Leicester), B. Webster (Seacombe), Davey (High Wycombe), G. H. Walker (Barrow), J. Parker (vice-president Lancashire County, Barrow), W. Anders (St. •Helens), J. Bickerstafie (Liverpool), C. Lyon (Liverpool), F. Wright (Widnes), W. Fernandez (Dewsbury), G. Kilner (Oldham), G. Thompser' (Chester), A. H. Speight (Hunslet), and T. Hudson, (Rochdale Hornets).---Mr. E. Mather, a Warringtonianr wired congratulations fromlGermany.</p>
        <p>THE CELEBRATlONS- 'Warrington's victory will be fittingly celebrated ill the course of the next few days. This (Saturday) even - lug, Mr. W. IL Niel (president) will entertain the CuP team le ague players, committee, and a few friends at dinner at the Lion Hotel. On Friday evening next, the townspeople will again be afforded an oppqii tanity of honouring the cup-holders. The team No; make a tour of the entire route on specially illuminatea cars, and the spectacle should be most effective. Several bands have offered their services. The celebrai tions were originally arranged for Wednesday, bil as the Mayor will be in London that day on Corpora- tion business, it was decided to hold them on FridaY• the Mayor will entertain the tn] i o 'own Hall. On Tuesday, May 23vd' aPnrdi or o fli t o officials al thes adtistphleay the Players andoificials will be the guests of Sir C4ilbert and Lady Greenall at a banquet!</p>
      </div>
