        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRING lON.— MUNICIPAL BYE-ELECTION 1905. TO MS BURGESSES OF ST. AUSTIN'S WARD, LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, Having been unanimously selected by the Conservative Committee and at the request of a huge circle of personal friends belonging to both political parties. I have con- sented to contest the Ward at the approaching Municipal Bye-Election. Being p determined advocate of economy in the spending of publia monies, I am of opinion that every step possible should be taken to further reduce the high rates which we at present have to pay, and I would give careful consideration to efficiency and progress in the management of the affairs of our Borough. With regard to the vital necessity for absolute probity in public work, I am opposed to any member of the Council benefiting In any way from his position. As a large ratepayer and resident in the Ward your Interests and mine are identical. I should always be at hand ready and willing to give my hesrtierit support to any suggestions or just complaintu from any of the rate- payers. In cono'ualon if you honour me by electing me as one of your representatives it shall be my study to serve you to the best of my ability in promoting your interests and those of the Borough in general. Believe me to remain. Ladles and Gentlemen. Tours taith,ullv, GEORGE WILLIAIi 21, Bold Street, Warrington, April, 19th, 1905.</p>
