        <p>A along message from Afthertoff Iron-Ox Tablets cured Mrs. health than for the past ten years. I cannot' say too much in praise of Iron-Ox. Tablets. They Gerrard cured her husband are the only medicine that ever really touched my 1 complaints. I only wish I had known about them cured and strengthened her long ago. They might have saved the lives of one' or two of my children. I have only one left, and it &gt; is to iron-Ox Tablets that I attribute his present, little son. splendid health." "Tell me all about his case.' Where is he now?" Mrs. Elizabeth Annie Gerrard, the wife of David "He is at school. From the first he was a weak Gerrard, an employe in a colliery in the Atherton and sickly child, which is perhaps hardly to be• district of Lancashire, is a comparatively young wondered atrememberinghow I was afflicted before woman. She has only been married six years. and after that time. My son suffered terribly from a During that period she has hid four children. The weak stomach. He could not take his food at all, first born is now five years of age, and the only one In a manner of speaking, and all my children have living, suffered from the same stomach and bowel com- plaint. Doctors did not cure them, and three of I them died, when very young. "But about your son now at school?" "Well, after I had gained so much strength and, health from taking the Iron-Ox Tablets. I thought I would try their effect on my boy. and about sir, weeks ago I began to give him the Tablets regularly and the Improvement in his condition was most marked. Before then he was weak and delicate, and had no appetite worthy of the name. But hasn't he ail appetite now My word! You should see him eat Nothing cames amiss to him. And he is as bright and jull of life, and has a nice colour in hi cheeks. I don't want to lose him, I can tell you. w i 'wish he was here for you to see him. He s still going on with the Tablets, and so am I. I should not, like to be without them." "And now let me tell you about my husb nd. Some weeks ago my husband was afflicted with colic. • He was ill in bed, and attended by a doctor. Little progress was made. He had no passage, and the doctor said he would have to undergo an operation.</p>
