        <p>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION. - Result of the Sale of Work. As a result of the sale of work held in connection with the Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay, on Friday and Saturday last,the sum of £B3 has been added to the organ fund. The members worked hard for the success of the venture and deserve a full measure of praise. At Friday's opening ceremony Miss G. Crosfield delivered an Interesting address in which she emphasised the value of music and discipline in education. The more they encouraged a love of music in England, she said, the more it would raise and refine the tone of the nation.. Then in regard to discipline, it brought order, cleanliness,obedience,hard-headedness and endurance,and cheerful comradeship between rich and poor alike, as well as self respect and practical patriotism.—Captain G. R. Crosfield presided and commended the object of the sale.—The proceeds for the day amounted to £47 15s. 7d. On Saturday afternoon the sale wasre-opened by Miss Simpson. Mr. W. ROBERTS. J.P., presided, and said that next to his own place of worship, there was no place in War- rington that was dearer in his affectations than the Workingmen's Mission. He had had friends, associates, and colleagues in-connection with that mission ever since the Roindation stone of the old chapel in Liver- pool-road was laid. During tbit last few years he had learnt the preciousness and value of real friendship. He often thought that if it were not for human friend- ships there was a great deal in life which would be very dull and dreary. The friendships associated with Sunday schools and places ,of worship were the truest in the world. He asked his hearers never to loosen the ties which bound them to their Sunday schools and churches. Most of them had had a day school education, and they greatly prized it, but notwithstanding the importance they attached to education, he believed it was the training giveli in the Sunday schools by godly men and women, which had such an influence on their lives that they could never forget it. He could speak from experience of the great love which the late Mr. John Crosiled had for the mission. They had many conversations about the place and he (the speaker) could testify how precious the mission was to Mr. Crosfield. He was delighted to hear of the good work which was being done by the friends at Bank Quay. To know the late Mr. Crosfield was to I love him, to honour and respect him. He (Mr. Roberts) never, knew a man so full of sympathy for suffering humanity. Alluding to the object of the sale, Mr. Roberts said they had now a beautiful place of worship and a magnificent school. He was glad to notice ' that they were almost comidetely equipped for the success- ful carrying on of the work. He hoped that before the , sale ended they wduld have realised sufficient to clear off the debt on the organ. (Applause). Mrs. SIMPSON, in the course of a neat speech, expressed admiration of the title "Workingmen's Mission." There was nothing more ennobling or grander than the life and character of an upright and industrious workingman. Such men were the backbone of the</p>
