        <p>NOVELTIES FPE MAY NON NHOWING. W. T. WILLIAMS' NEW COSTUMES, MILLINERY, DRESSES, COATS, &amp;?... &amp;c. , 4S, SANKEY STREET', wARRINGTONt. ..,. , : LOCAL NEWS. ~ 'Coming Events. The Rev. Sebastian Anderton, St. Helens, will .. preach to-morrow (Sunday) in the Golboree-street , Baptist Church.. , , The annual Warrington horse parade will be held 4. OD Saturday, May 20th. Three silver cups, two silver medals, and a number of diplomas are being offered ~...for competition, and an excellent parade should result. Vurther particulars will be found in our adiesrtising I,,eolumns. The Sunday school anniversary in connection with i' Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church will be celebrated 'to-morrow by the holding of special services. The Rev. J. Grange Bennett will preach both morning and -evening. -, Two meetings will be held next week under the :auspices of the Warrington Progressive Labour League. tOn Wednesday the meeting will be in the St. James's •'- School, Latchford, and on Thursday in St. Barnabas 'School, Bostock-street. The speakers will include '.2lr. H. Vivian (prospective candidate for BirkenheadY ,atnd Mr. J. Howes. Mr. A. H. erosfield will preside :.'-over both meetings. A brass band contest will be held on the football ~' ground, Wilderspool road, on Saturday May 13th, • when prizes to the value of £lOO will be competed for. 'The contest has been arranged by Messrs. Crosfield's band and has attracted a large number of well known . ombinations. . "PREMIER." CYCLES at 12 guineas with change : Speed gear ,eci.' imequalled.—J. S. GARLA.ND, $1 to : 55, Bridge at., VVarrington.—Advt. 'l'lle Infirmary and Dispensary. A special general meeting of the governors of the above institution was held at the Infirmary last i. Friday afternoon, Mr. J. C. Parr presiding, for the ' Purpose of appointing an honorary assistant medical Officer. Tbere were two candidates for the post, Dr. ,josepli and Dr. Anderson, both of Warrington. The ,iaPpoiatment was conferred upon Dr. Joseph. : H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is a boon for Tired , Toilers.—Advt. ._ New Police Arrangements. ' According to a new arrangement the Sankey sectio of the Warrington police division, including Cuerdley :Cankey and Great Sankey, Collin's Green and Burton- wood, now forms part of the Widnes police area, under Superintendent Strickland. The area now extends , from Garston on the one side to Warrington on the other. -Under the superintendent there will be one inspector, seven sergeants, thirty-five constables, and two weights and measures officers. LADrEs' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER 'CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. An Impudent Theft. At the Warrington Police Court yesterday (Friday), James Monks, 40, Mersey-street, and William Dunbobbin, Gas-street, two Corporation lamplighters were charged with stealing from the stores of Messrs. ItYlands Bras., Dalton Bank, 71 cwts. of yarn value 40 Bs.—Supeiintendent Monaghan said that on Thursday morning Mr. Henry Monks, foreman, 'missed the yarn, and the matter was reported to the Police, A visit was made to the Axpley rope walks -where the prisoners carried on business, and the yarn was found on the promises.—Prisoners were sent to goal for one month each. Owing to the high price of Hogs, Bacon has advanced id. to lid. per lb.—Advt. Assessment Committee.---Publican's Success- ful Appeal. Mr. R. Evans, on behalf of Mr. Payton, appealed at the meeting of the Warrington Assessment Committee, -held yesterday, against the assessment of the Blue Back Inn, Allen-street, at £7O gross, £5O 10s. rateable. Mr. Evans said the -sales had been reduced by three barrels a week on account of the stopping of the circus and the demolition of property in the neighbourhood. It was decided to reduce the assessment to £65 gross and £55 rateable. At the meeting Mr. A. J. Jolley was re-elected chairman, Mr. Billinge being appointed "Vice-chairman. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Adyt. The i, Y " Temperance Society. WA meeting of the "V " branch of the British r O s Temperance Association was held in Bold street Parlour on Thursday afternoon. In the nbsence bf Miss Dutton, Mrs. Simpson presided, and gave some interesting extracts from a diary, written while at a Convention held at Geneva. The secretary also read a letter received from Miss Kinsbuie-, who Poke so well at the Town Hall,wishing the new branch 'every success, and giving much useful advice. The next open meeting will be held on May 23rd (evening), when Mr. Spies.tie Halter has kindly consented to give a paper on Alcohol. The sale in aid of the funds of the Society will be held on June 22nd. , UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. *nlbulance Prize Distribution. , The presentation of medals and certificates to mem- IJers of the Stretton Ambulance Class, conducted by Pt!. Bower, took place on Thursday night. Mr. dlington distributed the prizes as follows :—Medal, Win. Holland. Second examination: J,, H. Darby- hire, J. Cobbley, First examination : T. Hartley, iti Meacock, T. Spafford, T. Kinsey, A. Povey, H. haddock, J. Cawley, M. Hopley, P. Chandler, and J. erry.—The Hon. and Rev. C. F. Cross presided, and }le and Mr. Billington were at the close of the pro- ceedings heartily thanked for their services. Owing to the high price of Hogs, bacon has ad- s sauced hi. to lid.per lb.—Advt. ale of Hackneys at Walton. Sir (iilbert Greenall, hart., held his annual sale by ction of pedigree hackneys, at Walton Hall, on itr hursday, when a large number of buyers attended culet all parts of the country. Mr. W. Bainbridge dig ,Posed of 66 lots comprising polo colts and ' shire geldings some £5,000 changing hands, in average t -13.1jais of £74 being realized. The highest price rptained was 335 guineas for "Yeoman," a dark nestnut gelding, foaled in 1901, which became the roperty of Mr. John Jones, Whitegate stud. Two 'a37 geldings became the property of Mr. Gabriel ,I),_ arufrees, for 160 guineas each. Other high prices ~,realized were :-150 guineas, paid by Mr. Sykes k lora three-year-old filly " Tennington Waggery," 110 100 guineas for two chesuut geldi4s, 115 guineas , ....;ereid"iagMe,adowland " a chestnut gelding ; 130 guineas -tor a six-eyear-old brown mare, 210 guineas fora brown Lord Roberts " 125 guineas for " Terring- -1?4 Vendetta " 135 guineas and 155 guineas for a olvn and a roan gelding, 185 guineas far" Dasher" %roan gelding, and 100 guineas for " Kettle" a rowe g3lding. The lowest price reached was six guineas for a brovin colt. ~,." PREMIER" CYCLES at 7 guineas startling value.— " S. GARLAND, 51 to 33, Bridge et, Warrington.— Advt. -Amu --iing Students' Association. Assoeiation had their fortaightly visit on , 4 Clrcisy last to Helsby. The secretary had arranged afternoon for the members. They first had a ,ier:: eltaib up the hill side to where Mr. Griffiths is mrvetitillg two villas. After carefully noting their con- . cotilen, and witnessing the method of clearing the , 1 , ailing ground by means of blasting they then to the Helsby ,Quarries where the most method of obtaining the stone from the quarry Teae4hiseen in operation. An American channelling , dee •ne cut a groove three inches wide and about 5f u. i 1 ' P M the rock; the workmen then split it off its bed , 4eans of wedge, the blocks of stone being thereby red with a minimum amount of fracture. Mr., th 5 owner of the quarries, informed us tha 1,401° Is only another machine in England of the same The company next visited the Helsby Cable ,71 v:„Li;, Where they were met by Mr. Barlow the who conducted them through there well and extensive works, where was seen the ions 'other processes of the manufacture of cables and -, eiab_aelectrical appliances, golf balls, &amp;c. The . trot"e7r,te machinery is in itself of great interest. A Itr• B '°l thanks to Mr. Barlow and to the Directors, , end Xte erton and Mr. Taylor' kind for the privilege o In erl Br . - tthe . students brought to a close one of the 1 —nat interesting meetings the Association have had. ,</p>
