n of the Council on it. Councillor Psmeessisfes: Would it not be better to settle the matter now? if you go to the General Purposes Committee the question will have to be settled again here. The M IYOR : But you can only speak once on it here whereas you can speak several times on committee. Councillor PEMBERTON: Let us discuss it now. ' The MAYOR: But it is not on the minutes now. It is withdrawn, and cannot be discussed. Alderman BENNETT : There is a great deal to be said about the matter, and I shall have my own views to express when the time comes, but it is not wise to go into details in public. The resolution was withdrawn and the other minutes a6•eed to. THE HONOUR AND INTEGRITY OF THE 1 COUNCIL. The General Purposes Committee have resolved that Alderman Pierpoint and Smethurst and Councillors Ashcroft, Clegg, Craik, Lord, and Stott be appointed a committee to investigate the charges made against a member in accordance with the resolution passed at tire last meeting of the Council,. and that the above com- mittee also be appointed to investigate a charge made against a member of the Council in relation to the Water Department and any other matter affecting the honour or integrity of the members of the Council. Councillor LORD said he had no recollection of the committee adding the sentence "and any other matter affecting the honour or integrity of the members of the Council." He would object to being a member of a , permanent committee to investigate any trumped up charges in the future. (Hear, hear). 1 The MAYOR: I hope they won't bring any trumped up charges. (Hear, hear.) Councillor LORD : Well, what is the meaning of that last sentence ? Alderman PIERPOTNT : I was present when the resolu- tion was passed, and I thought the latter part was quite understood and asreed upon. The MAYOR: I thought so too. We wanted to give the committee scope enough. Councillor LORD : It does not mean that there are any other charges. The MAYOR : Oh, no. Councillor STOTT : I hope there is no danger of the committee being a permanent one. (Laughter.) The resolution was approved. A motion by Councillor Lord authorizing the Investi- gation Committee to engage the services of a qualified shorthand writer to take down the evidence tendered at</p>
