        <p>BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME. ' BY 0 IA 3U:47 I . [COPYItIGHT,] xxxyt. Screw and Reverse Rotation (CONTINUED.) DIAGRAM 94.</p>
        <p>Some screw in-offs from a dead ball.—Cue ball at A 6A inches from the top cushion and 29 inches from the side cushion, and at B 6A inches from the top cushion and 32 inches from the side cushion. Red ball in both cases right behind the spot and touching the cushion. Position C, red ball touch- ing the side cushion and 30 inches from the top cushion. Cue ball 20 inches from the side cushion and 42 inches from the top cushion. IDiagram 94 shows the red ball tight up against the top cushion, and with the cue ball at A or at B, no in-off into the corner pocket would ap- peal- at all on to the majority of players. With the cue ball at A, however, an in-oil can be made by two totally different strokes. The first is a strong forcing stroke, with plenty of check side (left-hand side in the dia- gram.) This stroke, when the angle is a good one is not very difficult on a table with moderately easy pockets, but when the pockets are very small—and many standard tables have pockets smaller than required by the miles the stroke is hardly on, for the speed st which the ball strikes the angle of the side cushion (the opposing angle) causes it to he thrown away from the pocket. The tendency of the check side is, of course, to carry the ball into the pocket off the opposing angle. Although the object ball is touching the cushion, no its&amp; (or at least no perceptible kiss) takes place when the stroke is properly played, for a fast ball has time to get away before the object ball re- bounds from the cushion after having been pressed into it. The in-off is also on from Aby means of a thin screw, a much more scientific if a somewhat more difficult stroke than the forcing stroke. . The superiority of the thin screw per the forcing stroke lies in the fact that, whereas the, latter stroke Must be played at a high Dace with the conse- quent danger of the opposing angle throwing the ball away from the pocket, much less pace is requirsd for the former. Thus for a good player, the in-off by means of a thin screw would always be by far the more reliable stroke on a table with anything like tight pockets. , Though this thin screw in-off is always a high- *lass stroke, it should not be beyond the power of fairly good players. The cue ball should be struck low down with plenty of check side, and in the position shown on the diagram (cue ball at A) the object ball should be taken about quarter-ball. Another variation of this thin screw in-off is ihown with the cue ball at B. The stroke is much the same as before, the only difference being that the object ball must be taken a little fuller than when playing from A. The contact must, however, still be less than half ball. With the cue ball at B, the angle formed by the balls and the packet does not allow of the in-off being made by the forcing stroke. Another position for a screw in-off from a ball touching the cushion is shown at C. This is simply a variation of the position shown at B and as similar positions not infrequently occur, pretty good players would do well to practise these high-class strokes. •</p>
        <p>DIAGRAM 95. ~ 6Th .e. , , , - / * 0,....... lb," # * 0 V 1 e' N• 1 * a,/ V • , • e/ • A, 7,4,, . Si , ‘% • r'7/ (I I Ai 4.b. • --.... \ . •</p>
        <p>•V c .., • ; —,—,_—, . IllUr 7') I r, , : 1, i • , 1 .</p>
        <p>hack be played, putting as much strength Into the stroke as possible, the cue ball can be forced an inch or two over the line before it rushes back. In the ordinary way the cue would be held horizontally, or as horizontally as playing over the cushion and hitting the ball low down allows. If, however, the butt of the cue be con- siderably.elevated, and the stroke be played with great strength, the cue ball though hitting the object ball full in the face can be forced six or eight inches and even more over the baulk line before it rushes back to the striker. This stroke can therefore be made into a very pretty fancy stroke, a kind of combined follow-on and screw- back shot. As already explained in an earlier article, when the butt of the cue is considerably raised, the object ball should not be struck so low down as when screwing back with a horizontally held cue, otherwise the cue meets with little resist., once from the ball, and a miss-cue is the result., (To be Continued.) Next week a stroke considered impossible Ti many players will be described in this column, Vol. I. of "Billiards: The Strokes of thoi Game." 100 illustrations, By RISO LEVI, IS now ready, Is. net. Mr. Rico Levi will be pleased to answez,- in these columns all questions relative to the article appearing weekly, and as far as possible any questions on other points in connection with the game of Billiards, Envelopes should in parked "Billiards."</p>
        <p>H. A. MEADOWS (WARRINGTON).—Check side should only be used if the angle is narrow not otherwise. RISO LEVI. PRESGOT WORKHOUSE MASTER KILLED. Sad Tramway Fatality. On Saturdayafternoon a shocking fatality occurred on the St. Helens and District Tramway Company's service at Rainhill. As a car was leaving the Rainhill railway bridge to proceed to Prescot, by some means the trolley standard was pulled out and fell heavily on to the head of Mr. Williams, the master of the Prescot Union Workhouse, who was a passenger on top of the car, causing terrible injuries to the skull, and he died immediately. A doctor was close at hand, but his ser- vices were of no avail. Mr. Williams was appointed workhouse master in November, 1894.</p>
